WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.940
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.750
where we discuss science

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.827
and science-based tools for everyday life.

00:00:05.827 --> 00:00:09.220
[upbeat music]

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.210
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.060
and I'm a Professor of
Neurobiology and Ophthalmology

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.010
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.600
Today, we are going to discuss
the science and practice

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.780
of flexibility and stretching.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.050
Flexibility and stretching
are topics that I believe

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.370
do not receive nearly as much
attention as they deserve.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.710
For most people, the topics
of flexibility and stretching

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.200
bring to mind things like yoga,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.030
injury prevention, or
maybe even contortionism.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.340
But it turns out that
flexibility and stretching

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.090
are features that are built
into our basic body plan.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.290
Young children, young animals, and adults,

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.880
and, indeed, older children and animals

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.680
all stretch and all have
some degree of flexibility.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.490
It turns out that having flexibility,

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.150
and our ability to stretch,

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.190
and the interaction between
stretching and flexibility

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.720
are fundamental to how we move,

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.960
our ability to learn new movements,

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:04.170
indeed also to prevent
injury or repair injuries,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.120
and to offsetting and
reducing inflammation

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:07.953
throughout the body.

00:01:07.953 --> 00:01:09.530
In fact, today I'm going to share with you

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.410
a remarkable set of studies
that show that stretching

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.610
can actually adjust
things like tumor growth.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.760
This is work that was done
by one of the major directors

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.670
of the National Institutes of Health.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.180
So, today's discussion will start

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.740
with a description of the mechanisms,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.900
literally the cells

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.670
and the connections
from your nervous system

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.950
that mediate flexibility and stretching.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.980
And I promise that I'll make

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.990
that information accessible to you

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.860
whether or not you have a
biology background or not.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.700
Then with that information in hand,

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.820
I'm going to present to you
what the scientific literature

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.010
says about the best times
and ways to stretch,

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:43.300
everything right down to the detail

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.230
of how long to hold a stretch,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.640
whether or not to hold a stretch at all

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.917
because it turns out there are
multiple kinds of stretching.

00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:50.430
So, you can imagine you have stretches

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.100
where you hold the stretch
for a very long time

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.967
and use as little momentum as possible,

00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:57.010
and then there's also
what's called dynamic

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:57.950
and ballistic stretching

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:59.770
where you're literally swinging your limbs

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.730
trying to increase the range of motion.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.520
I will explain the science and application

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:04.590
of flexibility and stretching

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.330
in the context of sports performance,

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.960
whether or not you are engaging
in cardiovascular exercise,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.460
or resistance exercise, or both,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.960
whether or not you're
a competitive athlete

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.410
or simply a recreational
exerciser, as I am,

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.000
whether or not you are
trying to increase your range

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.560
of motion and flexibility
for longevity purposes,

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.080
or whether or not you're trying to do it

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.740
in order to access different
parts of your nervous system

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.400
'cause we'll soon learn today

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.520
that your ability to improve flexibility

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.000
and, indeed, to engage in
specific stretching exercises

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.310
can actually be used
to powerfully modulate

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.270
your ability to tolerate pain,

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.360
both emotional and physical pain.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.020
So, this thing that we call
flexibility and stretching

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.800
is actually a vast landscape.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.430
We're going to simplify and
organize all that for you today

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.120
and by the end of today's episode,

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.140
you're going to have a number
of simple, easy-to-apply tools

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.480
that are grounded in the
best scientific research

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.080
that you can apply for
your specific goals.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.790
Before we begin, I'd like to
emphasize that this podcast

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.780
is separate from my teaching
and research roles at Stanford.

