00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.510
Let's talk about
flexibility and stretching.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.280
Before we talk about the practices

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:28.817
of flexibility and stretching,

00:07:28.817 --> 00:07:30.850
I'd like to just highlight
some of the features

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.070
that are already built
into your nervous system

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:36.600
and into your body that
allow you to be flexible.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.910
Some of us feel tighter than others,

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:42.000
sometimes in specific
limbs or areas of our body,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.440
some people feel really loose and limb,

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.780
some people even have what's
called a hyper-flexibility.

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:50.440
I, for instance, have a relative
that can take her fingers

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:53.400
and bend them back to the point
where they touch her wrist.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.100
And it always makes me
cringe a little bit,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:57.500
but she can do that without any pain,

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.560
she seems to have some
hyper-flexibility in her joints.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:01.320
I do not have that feature.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.150
Some of you may find that
you are more flexible

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:04.550
than others naturally,

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.490
and some of you might be thinking

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.940
you don't need to build
in additional flexibility.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.680
Well, I think by the
end of today's episode,

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:14.280
you'll realize that almost
all of us can benefit

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.280
from having some sort of
understanding about flexibility

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.410
and having some stretching protocol

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.440
that we incorporate into our life,

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.450
if not just for physical
performance reasons

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.060
and for postural reasons,

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:29.600
then also for cognitive
and mental reasons,

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:32.150
and I'll be sure to clarify
what all of that means.

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:34.300
Right now, I'd like to take a moment

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.420
and just highlight the
flexibility that you already have.

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:40.450
For instance, if you were to move your arm

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:42.130
behind your torso a little bit

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.340
and then sort of let go

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.580
or stop exerting any effort in doing that,

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:49.520
you would find that the limb would return

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:51.450
more or less to a position
next to your torso,

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.610
at least I would hope so.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:54.480
And why is that?

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.060
Well, it turns out that there are aspects

00:08:56.060 --> 00:09:00.230
of your nervous system, aspects
of your skeletal system,

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:01.940
aspects of your muscles,

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:04.160
and aspects of the connective tissue

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.710
that binds all of that together,

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:09.350
that try and restore a particular order

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.710
or position to your limbs

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:14.290
and your limbs relative to one another.

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:17.580
So, that reflects a very
specific set of processes

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.120
that it turns out are
the same set of processes

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.440
that you use when you are trying

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.150
to enhance flexibility and stretching.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:25.840
So, I'd like to just take a moment

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:29.890
and review the basic
elements of nervous system,

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:34.660
muscle, connective tissue,
and skeletal tissue, bone,

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:38.060
that allow for flexibility and stretching.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.880
And here we can point
to two major mechanisms

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.620
by which your nervous system,

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.560
neurons, meaning nerve cells,

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.550
communicate with muscles,

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:50.240
and those muscles communicate
back to your nervous system

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.890
to make sure that your
limbs don't stretch too far,

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:56.540
they don't move too far
such that you get injured.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:58.720
And in addition to that,

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:01.840
mechanisms that ensure that
you don't overload your muscles

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.180
too much with weight, or
with tension, or with effort

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:06.730
and damage them that way.

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.870
'Cause it turns out that the
second security mechanism

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:11.950
of making sure that you
don't overload muscles

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:13.270
can be leveraged toward

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:16.670
increasing your flexibility
almost immediately.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:18.730
That's right, there
are protocols and tools

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:20.450
that I'll share with you
that are going to allow you

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:25.000
to vastly improve your
flexibility over time.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:26.780
But there are also
mechanisms that allow you

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:30.930
to quite significantly increase
your degree of flexibility

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.770
in a very short period of time,

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:34.420
and within just a few seconds.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:38.450
So, let's establish some of the
basic biological mechanisms.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.950
Any time we talk about
biology or physiology,

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.560
we're going to talk about structure,

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:44.240
meaning the cells and their connections,

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.350
and functions, what they do.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:47.940
There are just a few names to understand,

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:50.290
you do not have to memorize these names.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.120
The important thing that
I'd like you to know

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:54.600
is that flexibility and
the process of stretching

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:58.820
and getting more flexible
involves three major components,

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:01.230
neural, meaning of the nervous system,

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.310
muscular, muscles, and connective tissue.

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:05.530
Connective tissue is the stuff

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:09.450
that surrounds the neural
stuff and the muscular stuff,

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:11.110
although it's all kind of weaved together

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:13.380
and braided together in complicated ways.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.030
Some of you may have heard of fascia.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:16.760
We're going to talk a little
bit about fascia today,

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:19.040
although it's such an interesting tissue

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:22.009
that's really deserving
of its own episode.

00:11:22.009 --> 00:11:24.600
Fascial tissue, we're going to
talk about some of the stuff

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.440
that surrounds muscles that
really gives you your shape,

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:28.740
and holds everything together,

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.470
and allows for flexibility to occur.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.750
So, here's a key thing
that everyone should know

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:37.160
whether or not you're talking
about flexibility or not.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:40.240
Your nervous system controls your muscles,

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.780
it's what gets your muscles to contract.

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:44.320
So, within your spinal cord,

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.940
you have a category of
neurons, nerve cells,

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:48.980
that are called motor neurons.

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:51.200
To be precise, they
are lower motor neurons

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.530
'cause they're in your spinal cord.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:55.090
We call them lower to distinguish them

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:57.700
from the motor neurons
that are in your brain

00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:58.913
up in your skull.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:01.970
Those lower motor neurons,

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.870
hereafter I'll just refer
to them as motor neurons.

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:06.710
If I want to talk about the
other kind of motor neurons,

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:07.760
I'll say upper motor neurons.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:08.810
So, if I say motor neurons,

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:10.900
I just mean the ones in your spinal cord.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:14.260
Those motor neurons send a little wire

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:17.060
or set of wires out to your muscles,

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:20.420
and that creates what's called
a neuromuscular junction,

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:22.390
which just means that the
neurons meet the muscles

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:23.930
at a particular place.

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:25.920
Those neurons release a chemical,

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:29.020
that chemical is called acetylcholine.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.120
Some of you may have heard
about acetylcholine before,

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:33.660
acetylcholine also exists in your brain

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:35.150
and does other things in your brain,

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:38.200
mainly it's involved
in focus and attention.

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:40.230
But at the neuromuscular junction,

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:43.160
the release of acetylcholine
from these nerve cells,

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:45.010
these neurons, onto the muscles

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:46.820
causes the muscles to contract.

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:47.960
And when muscles contract,

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:51.770
they are able to move limbs

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:55.990
by way of changing the
length of the muscle,

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:59.090
adjusting the function
of connective tissue

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:00.630
like tendons and ligaments.

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:01.570
And for instance,

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:04.070
if you're bringing your wrist
closer to your shoulder,

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:08.200
that biceps muscle is
contracting, it's getting shorter.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.700
I mean, in reality it hasn't
gotten shorter overall,

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:13.170
it's just temporarily shorter, of course.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:15.960
All of that is controlled by neurons.

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:18.750
And it's those motor
neurons from the spinal cord

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.750
that are really responsible
for the major movement

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:25.500
of your limbs by way
of causing contraction

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.670
of specific muscles at specific times.

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:30.560
So, the key thing to
take away is that nerve

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.400
controls the contraction of muscles.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.450
Now, within the muscles themselves,

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:39.340
there are nerve connections.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.440
And these are nerve connections that arise

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:43.540
from a different set of
neurons in the spinal cord

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:45.780
that we call sensory neurons.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.260
The sensory neurons

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:49.390
exist in a different
part of the spinal cord,

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:53.100
and they send a low wire or
set of wires into the muscles.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.880
And there's a particular
kind of sensory neuron

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:58.440
that comes out of your spinal
cord and into your muscles,

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:01.900
which are called spindle neurons.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:06.410
They create or they actually
wrap around muscle fibers,

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:08.050
kind of corkscrew around them

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:10.120
and give kind of a spring-like appearance.

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:12.180
And for you aficionados out there,

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.910
these are intrafusal
connections or neurons.

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:18.080
Intrafusal means within the muscle,

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:19.870
but you really don't need to know that

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:21.770
unless you're really curious about it,

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:23.110
or you're going to
become a neuroscientist,

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:25.370
or you're in medical school or something.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:29.870
These spindle connections
within the muscle

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:31.620
that wrap around the muscle fibers

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:35.830
sense the stretch of those muscle fibers.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:39.300
So, now we have two parts to
the system that I've described.

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:40.360
You've got motor neurons

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:43.450
that can cause muscles
to contract and shorten,

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:48.240
and we have these spindles
within the muscles themselves

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:50.270
that wrap around the muscle fibers,

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:53.210
and that information
is sent from the muscle

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.180
back to the spinal cord.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:58.790
It's a form of sensing what's
going on in the muscle.

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:01.130
Much in the same way that
you have neurons in your eye

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:03.980
that sense light in your
external environment,

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:06.870
you have neurons in your
ear that sense sound waves

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:08.350
in your external environment,

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.760
you have neurons in your spinal cord

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:12.180
that are sensory neurons

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:16.570
that are sensing the amount
of stretch in the muscles.

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:21.120
What happens is if a given
muscle is stretching really far,

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:24.890
those sensory neurons, those
spindles within the muscle

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:28.900
will activate and will send
a electrical potential,

00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:30.600
literally a bit of electricity

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:33.420
along that wire's length
into the spinal cord.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:35.190
And then, within the spinal cord

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:37.520
that sensory neuron communicates

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:39.170
through a series of intermediate steps,

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:42.210
but to the motor neuron

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:45.220
and makes sure that that
motor neuron contracts.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:46.720
Now, why would that be useful?

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.270
Well, what this does is
it creates a situation

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:52.630
where if a muscle is or
is stretching too much

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:56.800
because the range of motion of
a limb is increased too much

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:58.490
then the muscle will contract

00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:03.490
to bring that limb range of
motion into a safe range again.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.170
Now, what determines whether
or not a range of motion

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:08.320
is quote-unquote safe or not

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:10.160
is dictated by a number of things.

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:11.760
It's dictated by things that are happening

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:13.760
in this kind of loop of neural connections

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.190
in the spinal cord and muscle.

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:18.450
It's also determined by
what's going on in your head,

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:20.170
literally in your mind cognitively

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:22.670
about whether or not the
movement of that limb

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:25.460
its increasing range of
motion is good for you,

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:27.160
whether or not you're
doing it deliberately,

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:28.190
whether or not it's bad for you.

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:31.390
And then there are also
some basic safety mechanisms

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:33.840
that are put in there that
really try and restrict

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:35.830
our limb range of motion.

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:37.610
Okay, so just to clarify,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.090
this whole thing looks like a loop,

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:40.670
and the essential components of the loop

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:43.543
are motor neurons contract muscles,

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:47.140
sensory neurons, of
which there are a bunch

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:47.973
of different varieties,

00:16:47.973 --> 00:16:49.590
well, in this case, what
we're calling the spindles

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.600
are sensing stretch within the muscles.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:55.120
And if a given muscle is elongating

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:58.810
because of the increased
range of motion of a limb,

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:01.110
those sensory neurons
send an electrical signal

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:02.440
into the spinal cord

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:05.570
such that there is an
activation of the motor neuron,

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:07.720
which by now should make perfect sense

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:08.730
as to why that's useful.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:10.720
It then shortens up the muscle.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:11.990
It actually doesn't
really shorten the muscle,

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:13.090
but it contracts the muscle

00:17:13.090 --> 00:17:17.110
that brings the limb back
into a safe range of motion.

00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:21.500
Okay, so this process is very fast,

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:25.970
it was designed to keep
your body together and safe.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:28.610
It's designed to make sure
that you don't take your arm

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:29.860
and swing it behind your torso

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:30.980
and it just goes all the way back

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:31.880
to the middle of your back.

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:33.140
I mean, unless you're a contortionist

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:35.590
or you've trained that kind
of level of flexibility,

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:36.423
that would be terrible

00:17:36.423 --> 00:17:40.010
because it could provide a
lot of damage to the muscles,

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.920
and to the connective
tissue, and so forth.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:46.420
So, that's one basic mechanism
that we want to hold in mind,

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:48.530
this idea of a spindle that senses stretch

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:50.520
and can activate
contraction of the muscles

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:51.780
and shorten the muscles.

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:53.670
The next mechanism I want to describe,

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:55.370
and once again there are only two

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:58.360
that you need to hold in
mind for this episode.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:01.030
This other mechanism has
a lot of the same features

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:02.680
as the one I just described,

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:04.820
but it has less to do with stretch,

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:06.210
in fact, it doesn't
have to do with stretch

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:09.770
as much as it has to
do with sensing loads.

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:14.570
So, at the end of each muscles
you have tendons typically,

00:18:14.570 --> 00:18:19.570
and there are neurons that
are closely associated

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.180
with those tendons

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:23.690
that are called Golgi
tendon organs, right?

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:26.440
These are neurons that are sensory neurons

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:29.300
that sense how much load
is on a given muscle.

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:31.970
Right, so if you're lifting
up something very, very heavy,

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:33.730
these neurons are going to fire,

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:35.330
meaning they're going to
send electrical activity

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:36.670
into the spinal cord.

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:41.170
And then, those neurons have
the ability to shut down,

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:44.320
not activate, but shut down motor neurons

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:47.830
and to prevent the
contraction of a given muscle.

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:49.750
So, for instance, if you were to walk over

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:53.920
and try and pick up a weight
that is much too heavy for you,

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:56.470
meaning you could not do it
without injuring yourself

00:18:56.470 --> 00:19:00.460
and you start to try and heave
that weight off the ground.

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:01.430
There are a number of reasons

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:02.680
why you might not be able to lift it,

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:03.750
but let's say you start to get it

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:04.970
a little bit off the ground,

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:08.380
or you start to get some force generated

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:10.300
that would allow it to move.

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.590
But the force that you're generating

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:15.440
could potentially rip your
muscles or your tendons

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:16.700
off of the bone,

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:18.067
right, that it could disrupt the joints

00:19:18.067 --> 00:19:20.030
and it could tear ligaments.

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:21.770
Well, you have a safety
mechanism in place,

00:19:21.770 --> 00:19:23.080
it's these Golgi tendon organs,

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:25.270
these GTOs, as they're called,

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:27.620
they get activated and
shut down the motor neurons

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:30.920
and make it impossible for
those muscles to contract.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:31.880
Okay, so on the one hand,

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:33.760
we have a mechanism that senses stretch

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:36.750
and can figure out when
stretch is excessive.

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:40.460
And when this system detects
that stretch is excessive,

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:43.363
it activates the contraction of muscles.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:47.600
And then, we have a second
mechanism that senses loads,

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:51.180
and when tension or
loads is deemed excessive

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:52.130
by these circuits,

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:53.500
and remember these
circuits don't have a mind,

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:55.060
they don't go, "Oh, this is excessive,"

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:56.240
they just sense loads.

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:58.650
And when those loads
exceed a certain threshold,

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:02.030
well then those GTOs,
those Golgi tendon organs,

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:03.450
send signals into the spinal cord

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:06.370
that shut down your motor neuron's ability

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:08.670
to contract muscle so that
you no longer can lift

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:09.760
that heavy load.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:12.260
So, both of these are
protective mechanisms,

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:16.210
but both of these can be
leveraged in a very logical way

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:17.490
and in a very safe way

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:20.250
in order to increase your
limb range of motion.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:22.030
So, there are a couple of
things I want to point out

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:24.170
before going a little bit further

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:25.890
into how your nervous system

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:28.280
controls flexibility and stretching,

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:31.000
and those key points are the following.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:34.250
There are now dozens if
not hundreds of studies

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:38.870
that show that a dedicated
stretching practice

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:41.240
can improve limb range of motion.

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:42.600
Now, for many of you listening,

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.000
you're probably saying, "Duh,"

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:46.330
but I think it's important
to point that out,

00:20:46.330 --> 00:20:48.250
that a dedicated stretching practice

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.600
can increase limb range of motion.

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:52.020
And as you'll soon learn,

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:55.150
there are specific mechanisms
that can explain that effect.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:57.610
The second point is one of longevity.

00:20:57.610 --> 00:20:59.010
And when I say longevity,

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:02.350
I don't necessarily mean late-stage aging.

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:06.270
We all undergo a decrease
in limb range of motion,

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:09.150
unless we do something
to offset that decrease.

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.780
And the current numbers
vary from study to study,

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:13.380
but if you look en masse,

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:14.640
you look at all of those studies

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:16.020
and what you basically find

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:19.720
is that we start to experience
a decrease in flexibility

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:24.560
from about age 20 until about
age 49 that's pretty dramatic.

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:27.700
And then, of course, it
will continue after age 49,

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:31.000
but basically it's a 10%
decrease every 10 years.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.760
So, we could say it's
a 1% decrease per year,

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:35.560
although it's not necessarily linear.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:36.393
What do I mean by that?

00:21:36.393 --> 00:21:39.650
Well, it's not necessarily
that on your 21st birthday,

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:41.720
you are 1% less flexible

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:43.170
than you were on your 20th birthday

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:45.290
and it decreased by 1% per year,

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:47.210
some of these changes can be non-linear.

00:21:47.210 --> 00:21:48.250
So, you can imagine the person

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:50.720
who's doing just fine
in terms of flexibility

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:51.860
between 20 and 30,

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:53.960
and then they get to 32

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:56.250
and suddenly they've lost
5% of their flexibility.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:58.810
Now, of course, there will be
a ton of lifestyle factors.

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:00.610
If you're a regular practitioner of yoga,

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:02.390
if you have a dedicated
stretching practice,

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:03.420
if you're doing other things

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.880
to improve your muscle contractibility,

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:07.410
so you're doing resistance training

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:11.770
it turns out can actually
indirectly improve flexibility.

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:13.400
There are a number of different factors,

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:15.860
but the key point is that maintaining

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:17.460
some degree of flexibility

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:21.420
and maybe even enhancing range
of motion and flexibility

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:23.970
is of immense benefit
for offsetting injury

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:26.740
provided it's not pushed too far.

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:27.690
There are a number of people

00:22:27.690 --> 00:22:30.070
who have pushed their limb range of motion

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:33.860
so far that they experience
all sorts of injuries,

00:22:33.860 --> 00:22:35.540
both acute and chronic injuries.

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:38.470
Today, we'll also talk about
how to avoid those scenarios.

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:40.200
Okay, so we've established
that there are mechanisms

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:42.770
within the spinal cord,
muscles, and connective tissue,

00:22:42.770 --> 00:22:45.130
those remember it's the motor neurons,

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:47.140
the spindles, the GTOs,

00:22:47.140 --> 00:22:49.410
and, of course, the muscles themselves,

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:51.810
and connective tissue, tendons,

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:54.230
but also other forms of connective tissue

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:57.120
that establish whether or not a limb

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:59.940
is going to stay within a
particular range of motion or not,

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:03.220
and whether or not a limb
is going to be allowed

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:06.070
by the nervous system to pursue

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.883
or handle a given load, a given tension.

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:12.540
There are also mechanisms that arrive

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:14.770
to the neuromuscular system

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:18.160
from higher up in the nervous
system, from the brain.

00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:21.100
And those mechanisms involve
a couple of different facets

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:23.080
that are really interesting,

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:25.010
and I think that we should all know about.

00:23:25.010 --> 00:23:27.330
In fact, today, I'm going to
teach you about a set of neurons

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:30.650
that I'm guessing 99.9% of
you have never heard of,

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:32.960
including all you
neuroscientists out there,

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:36.460
if you're out there, and
I know you're out there,

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:40.580
that seem uniquely enriched in humans

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:43.300
and probably perform essential roles

00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:45.480
in our ability to regulate our physiology

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:47.070
and our emotional state.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:48.170
So, within the brain,

00:23:48.170 --> 00:23:51.340
we have the ability to sense
things in the external world,

00:23:51.340 --> 00:23:53.370
something we called exteroception,

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:55.080
and we have the ability to sense things

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:58.680
in our internal world within
our body, called interoception.

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:01.380
Interoception can be the
volume of food in your gut,

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:02.520
whether or not you're experiencing

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:04.180
any organ pain or discomfort,

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:06.620
whether or not you feel good
in your gut and in your organs,

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:08.850
that's actually a kind of
feeling, "Hmm, I feel great,

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:10.960
I feel sated, I feel relaxed,"

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:14.010
but those are all different
forms of interoception.

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:16.470
The main brain area that's associated

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:18.560
with interpreting what's
going on in our body

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:21.270
is called the insula, I-N-S-U-L-A.

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:22.810
It's a very interesting brain region,

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:24.170
it's got two major parts.

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:29.170
The front of it is mainly
concerned with things like smell,

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:33.700
and to some extent vision, and
to some extent other things

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:36.230
that are arriving from the external world

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:39.030
and combining with what's
going on internally

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:41.140
and making sense of all that,

00:24:41.140 --> 00:24:43.160
or at least routing that
information elsewhere

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:44.740
in your nervous system to make decision,

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:46.500
like if you smell something
good to approach it,

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:48.280
or if you smell something bad to avoid it.

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:50.270
The front of the insula is really doing

00:24:50.270 --> 00:24:53.750
all of that kind of stuff
along with other brain areas.

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:57.870
The posterior insula, the
back of the insula that is,

00:24:57.870 --> 00:25:01.800
has a very interesting and
distinct set of functions.

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:04.420
The posterior insula is mainly concerned

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:08.010
with what's going on with
your somatic experience.

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:09.490
How do you feel internally?

00:25:09.490 --> 00:25:13.820
And how is the movement
that you happen to be doing

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.590
combining with your internal state

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:19.160
to allow you to feel,

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:22.120
as I like to say the nervous
system mainly batches things

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:25.060
into yum, like, "Oh, this
is really good for me,"

00:25:25.060 --> 00:25:27.630
yuck, "This is really bad
for me and I need to stop,"

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:29.760
or meh, "This is kind of neutral."

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:31.330
Okay, so this isn't about food,

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:33.440
but we could say for most stimuli,

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:35.500
most senses, whether or not they're senses

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:37.580
of things internally or externally,

00:25:37.580 --> 00:25:39.400
our nervous system is
trying to make decisions

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:40.840
about what to do with that information,

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:43.390
and so it mainly batches
information into yum,

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:46.100
I want to keep doing this
or approach this thing,

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:50.520
or continue down some path
of movement, or eating,

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:53.610
or staying in a temperature
environment, et cetera,

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:55.270
or yuck, I need to get out of here,

00:25:55.270 --> 00:25:56.400
I don't want any more of this,

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:57.440
I don't want to keep doing this,

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:00.000
this is painful, or
aversive, or stressful.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.200
And then meh, so if it
doesn't really matter,

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:03.760
I can just kind of stay right here or not.

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:05.460
Yum, yuck, and meh.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:09.900
Well, in your posterior insula,

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:11.990
you have a very interesting population

00:26:11.990 --> 00:26:13.540
of very large neurons,

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:16.030
these are exceptionally large neurons

00:26:16.030 --> 00:26:18.750
called von Economo neurons.

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:21.180
These are neurons that are, again,

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:22.970
unbeknownst to most neuroscientists

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:26.430
and they seem uniquely enriched in humans.

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:28.080
Chimpanzees have them

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:30.270
and some other large animals have them.

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:32.230
So, they're found in whales,

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:35.610
chimpanzees, elephants, and in humans.

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:38.690
But even though we are much
smaller than most whales

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:41.590
and even though we are much
smaller than most elephants,

00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:43.510
I mean, remember there are baby elephants.

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:45.520
As far as I know, they haven't bred up

00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:47.100
like mini-elephants yet,

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:49.140
they seem to have a teacup version

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:50.983
of pretty much every dog breed.

00:26:52.121 --> 00:26:52.992
You can look that up,

00:26:52.992 --> 00:26:55.840
I certainly have mixed
feelings about this notion

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:57.590
of trying to downsize everything

00:26:57.590 --> 00:26:58.490
to the point where you could

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:00.180
kind of like the pocket-sized bulldog

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:01.390
I think of someday will arrive.

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:03.087
I'm not a fan of that kind of downsizing

00:27:03.087 --> 00:27:04.570
of different breeds,

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:06.460
but because there aren't teacup elephants

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:10.300
and teacup gorillas, and teacup
chimpanzees, and so forth,

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:13.140
most all of those other
species are larger than us.

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:15.930
They have these von Economo neurons

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:18.060
and we have these von Economo neurons,

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:22.460
but we have in upwards
of 80,000 of these things

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:24.050
in our posterior insula.

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:25.600
These other species tend to have

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:29.230
somewhere in the range of
1,000 to maybe 10,000 or so.

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:30.810
Why is that interesting?

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:34.010
Well, these von Economo neurons

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:36.940
have the unique property of integrating

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:40.810
our knowledge about our body movements,

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:43.290
our sense of pain and discomfort,

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:45.700
and can drive motivational processes

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:48.030
that allow us to lean into discomfort

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:50.500
and, indeed, to overcome any discomfort

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:53.140
if we decide that the discomfort
that we are experiencing

00:27:53.140 --> 00:27:57.060
is good for us or directed
toward a specific goal.

00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:58.070
This knowledge turns out

00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:59.760
to be very important to keep in mind

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:01.920
because as we migrate this conversation

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:03.360
toward the things that we can do

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:05.423
to enhance flexibility and stretching,

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:08.520
you'll soon learn that there are moments

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:10.080
within a stretching protocol

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:13.640
where you have the opportunity
to either override pain

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:16.330
and discomfort to kind of relax through it

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:17.970
or push through it.

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:21.240
Right, there's a decision
fork in the road there,

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:23.890
and it'll tell you which
fork in the road to take,

00:28:23.890 --> 00:28:26.740
or to say, "Uh-uh, I'm
not going to do that.

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:28.750
I'm going to allow these natural reflexes

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:30.080
of the spindle to kick in

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.520
and just essentially
stop me from stretching

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:34.670
if a given limb isn't designed

00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:37.420
or shouldn't be stretched that far."

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:41.223
So, I'd like you to keep these
von Economo neurons in mind.

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:44.270
I should mention they're named von Economo

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:46.450
because the guy, Constantin von Economo,

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:51.210
that discovered them at the
end of the 1800s, early 1900s,

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:54.130
decided to name them after
himself, as many scientists do,

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:56.870
or certainly the
neurologists and physicians

00:28:56.870 --> 00:28:58.890
are famous for naming
things after themselves.

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:00.550
These von Economo neurons

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:02.730
turn out to be very
important to keep in mind

00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:04.780
as we embark on our exploration

00:29:04.780 --> 00:29:08.150
of what sorts of stretching practices

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:11.550
can be best applied to
increase flexibility

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:15.700
because whether or not
you undertake a mild,

00:29:15.700 --> 00:29:19.590
moderate, or intense flexibility training,

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:23.540
you will no doubt encounter
a scenario at some point

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:27.390
where you will have to ask
yourself, "Do I quote-unquote

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:29.380
relax into this stretch,

00:29:29.380 --> 00:29:31.260
or do I try and push through

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.060
just a little bit of discomfort?"

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:35.450
And I'll explain how
to gauge that decision

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:38.930
in a very specific and ideally safe way,

00:29:38.930 --> 00:29:40.300
and I'll give you some
tools that will allow you

00:29:40.300 --> 00:29:41.750
to make that decision in a way

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:44.900
that best preserves the integrity
of those neural circuits

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:47.590
that I described earlier
and can keep you safe.

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:51.810
These von Economo neurons
sit in the exact position

00:29:51.810 --> 00:29:54.230
that one would want to be able to evaluate

00:29:54.230 --> 00:29:55.870
what's going on in the body,

00:29:55.870 --> 00:29:58.410
in particular what's going on
in terms of limb movements,

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:00.690
how that relates to our
feelings of discomfort.

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:03.610
And then there's the other aspect

00:30:03.610 --> 00:30:05.010
of these von Economo neurons,

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:07.830
which is that these von Economo neurons

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:10.830
are connected to a number
of different brain areas

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:12.780
that can shift our internal state

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:15.190
from one of so-called
sympathetic activation.

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:19.580
So, this is a pattern of
alertness and even stress,

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:20.600
sometimes even panic,

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:23.080
but typically alertness and stress

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:26.710
to one of so called
parasympathetic activation

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:28.853
to one of relaxation.

00:30:30.290 --> 00:30:32.300
Oftentimes, you'll hear that stretching

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:34.900
should be done by
relaxing into the stretch.

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:37.710
Well, what does it actually
mean to relax into the stretch?

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:39.770
Well, these von Economo neurons

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:42.340
sit at this junction where
they're able to evaluate

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:44.490
what's going on inside our body

00:30:44.490 --> 00:30:47.020
and allow us to access neural circuitries

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:50.930
by which we can shift our
relative level of alertness

00:30:50.930 --> 00:30:55.270
down a bit, or our relative
level of stress down a bit

00:30:55.270 --> 00:30:59.600
and thereby to increase so-called
parasympathetic activation

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:04.420
and to literally override some
of those spindle mechanisms,

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:05.880
even the GTO mechanisms,

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:08.370
but especially the spindle mechanisms

00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:12.900
at the neuromuscular and
muscular spinal junction.

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:13.963
And in that way,

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:18.430
gently, subtly override the reflex

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:19.450
that would otherwise cause us

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:21.000
to contract those muscles back.

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:25.380
The reason that's possible
is because your brain

00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:27.040
has those other kinds of motor neurons,

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:30.750
the upper motor neurons
that can both direct,

00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:34.973
meaning control, and can
override lower motor neurons.

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.360
I'll give you a brief example of this

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:39.030
that you've already done in your life,

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:41.650
and that we all have the capacity for.

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:45.640
What I'm referring to is the
monosynaptic stretch reflex.

00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:48.740
This is something that every
first year neuroscience

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:50.040
graduate student learns,

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:51.900
which is that if you were to step

00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:54.620
on a sharp object with a bare foot,

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:56.780
you would not need to make the decision

00:31:56.780 --> 00:32:00.010
to retract your foot, you
would automatically do that

00:32:00.010 --> 00:32:02.180
provided you have a
healthy nervous system.

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:03.890
There are mechanisms in place

00:32:03.890 --> 00:32:06.720
that cause the retraction of that limb

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:10.610
by way of ensuring that
the proper muscles contract

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:13.060
and other muscles do not contract,

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:14.580
in fact, that they fully relax.

00:32:14.580 --> 00:32:17.510
Okay, so in the case of
stepping on a sharp object

00:32:17.510 --> 00:32:19.840
like a piece of glass,
or a nail, or a tack,

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:22.120
you would essentially
activate the hip flexor

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:25.540
to lift up your foot
as quickly as possible.

00:32:25.540 --> 00:32:29.700
In doing so, that same neural circuit

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:32.260
would activate a contralateral,

00:32:32.260 --> 00:32:35.000
meaning opposite side of the body circuit,

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:36.540
to ensure that the leg,

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:39.200
the foot that's not
stepping on the sharp object

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:41.800
would do exactly the
opposite and would extend

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:44.640
to make sure that you don't fall over.

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:46.570
All of that happens reflexively,

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:50.280
it does not require any
thought or decision making.

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:54.620
In fact, humans without any neocortex,

00:32:54.620 --> 00:32:56.610
literally that who decerebrate

00:32:57.780 --> 00:32:59.680
or an animal that doesn't have...

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:02.503
When I say decerebrate, I
mean lack of cerebral cortex.

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:05.440
They can perform that

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:07.160
because it's all controlled by circuits

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:11.310
that are basically below the
brain and in the spinal cord.

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:12.810
There's a little bit of
activation of circuits

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:14.480
in the kind of deeper parts of the brain,

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:16.340
but basically you don't need to think

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:18.230
or decide in order to do that.

00:33:18.230 --> 00:33:21.500
However, if your life depended

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:25.070
on walking across some
sharp objects, let's say.

00:33:25.070 --> 00:33:26.420
Let's make it a little less dramatic

00:33:26.420 --> 00:33:28.250
so it's not like the "Die
Hard" movie or something

00:33:28.250 --> 00:33:30.890
where he has to run
barefoot across the glass,

00:33:30.890 --> 00:33:32.660
although that's a pretty good example

00:33:32.660 --> 00:33:34.110
of what I'm describing here,

00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:37.460
but let's say you had to walk
across some very hot stones

00:33:37.460 --> 00:33:40.910
to get away from something
that you wanted to avoid.

00:33:40.910 --> 00:33:43.920
You could override that stretch reflex

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:46.500
by way of a decision made
with your upper motor neurons,

00:33:46.500 --> 00:33:47.920
your insula, and your cognition,

00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:50.130
and almost certainly
those von Economo neurons,

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:51.530
which would be screaming, "Don't do this,

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:52.460
don't do this, don't do this,"

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:54.940
could shuttle that
information to brain areas

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:57.490
that would allow you
to override the reflex

00:33:57.490 --> 00:33:59.520
and essentially push through the pain.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:01.590
And maybe even, in fact,

00:34:01.590 --> 00:34:03.980
even not experience the pain

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:06.480
to the same degree or even at all.

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:08.370
So, these von Economo neurons

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:11.323
sit at a very important
junction within the brain.

00:34:12.160 --> 00:34:14.550
They pay attention to what's
going on in your body,

00:34:14.550 --> 00:34:17.620
pain, pleasure, et cetera.

00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:19.510
And that includes what's
going on with your limbs

00:34:19.510 --> 00:34:21.240
and your limb range of motion.

00:34:21.240 --> 00:34:23.590
They also are paying attention

00:34:23.590 --> 00:34:27.150
and can control the amount of activation,

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:28.610
kind of alertness or calmness

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:32.490
that you are able to
create within your body

00:34:32.490 --> 00:34:35.560
in response to a given sensory experience.

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:37.370
And as I mentioned before,

00:34:37.370 --> 00:34:39.500
they seem to be uniquely
enriched in humans,

00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:43.930
they seem to be related to
the aspects of our evolution

00:34:43.930 --> 00:34:46.180
that allow us to make decisions

00:34:46.180 --> 00:34:47.870
about what to do with our body

00:34:47.870 --> 00:34:50.610
in ways that other
animals just simply can't.

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:51.560
Before we go any further,

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:53.460
I want to give you a practical tool

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:55.330
that you can, of course, use,

00:34:55.330 --> 00:34:58.880
but that will also give
you insight and experience

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:03.420
into your muscle spindle
spinal cord circuit mechanisms.

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:05.550
So, what I'd like you to do

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:07.890
is if you're in a proper place to do this,

00:35:07.890 --> 00:35:10.300
you're going to stand with legs straight,

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:11.780
meaning knees not bent,

00:35:11.780 --> 00:35:13.910
and you're going to try
and touch your toes,

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:16.300
or for some of you that's
going to be very easy

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:18.150
and you might even be
able to put your hands

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:20.090
flat on the floor.

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:20.923
I don't have that kind of flexibility,

00:35:20.923 --> 00:35:23.180
it's pretty easy for me to touch my toes.

00:35:23.180 --> 00:35:24.930
I don't care if you
round your back or not,

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:27.790
although ideally I would
say don't round your back.

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:29.790
Not because it's bad to do so necessarily,

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:32.360
but just to try and keep this

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:34.890
the same from trial to trial, as it were.

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:37.810
So, try and get a sense of
what your range of motion

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:40.610
is in terms of bending over at the waist

00:35:40.610 --> 00:35:41.900
while maintaining a flat back

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:43.990
and trying to touch your
toes or even touch the floor.

00:35:43.990 --> 00:35:46.500
Maybe, again, you can even
go hands flat to the floor

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:48.820
or maybe even far out in front of you.

00:35:48.820 --> 00:35:52.280
Okay, now what I'd like
you to do is stand back up

00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:56.820
and I'd like you to
contract your quadriceps

00:35:56.820 --> 00:36:01.290
as hard as you possibly
can for about 5-15 seconds.

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:02.340
Let's say 10 seconds

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:04.910
just to keep things
more or less normalized.

00:36:04.910 --> 00:36:08.170
This obviously is not a
super controlled experiment.

00:36:08.170 --> 00:36:09.550
So, to contract your quadriceps

00:36:09.550 --> 00:36:10.460
for those of you who don't know,

00:36:10.460 --> 00:36:13.360
you're going to extend
your lower limb out.

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:14.660
So, this would be like kicking,

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:15.780
although don't do it too quickly,

00:36:15.780 --> 00:36:18.970
you're going to kick out your foot,

00:36:18.970 --> 00:36:20.810
you should feel your quadriceps contract

00:36:20.810 --> 00:36:22.060
on the top of your thighs.

00:36:22.060 --> 00:36:24.770
And you're going to try and
consciously contract them

00:36:24.770 --> 00:36:26.300
as hard as you can.

00:36:26.300 --> 00:36:28.990
Okay, typically if you
want to point your toe

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:31.560
back towards your knee or
shin that's also going to help

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:34.560
somewhat to contract
even harder and harder.

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:36.913
Okay, so, do that for about 10 seconds.

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:40.070
A lot of you will do
this just while standing,

00:36:40.070 --> 00:36:41.110
contract, contract, contract.

00:36:41.110 --> 00:36:42.660
Okay, then release it,

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:47.180
and then now go ahead
and repeat that stretch

00:36:47.180 --> 00:36:49.490
where you're trying to touch
your toes or touch the floor.

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:51.520
So, this is, again, relying more or less

00:36:51.520 --> 00:36:54.680
on hamstring flexibility
among other things.

00:36:54.680 --> 00:36:56.840
Okay, what most of you will find

00:36:56.840 --> 00:36:59.590
is that you have an immediate increase

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:01.100
in hamstring flexibility,

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:04.330
or your range of motion has increased.

00:37:04.330 --> 00:37:05.610
If you didn't experience that,

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:06.450
then I would encourage you

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:09.410
to try and contract your
quadriceps harder and longer,

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:11.060
so maybe 20 or 30 seconds,

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:13.793
and then try this
so-called experiment again.

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:17.320
Why would contracting your quadriceps

00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:21.880
allow your hamstring flexibility
to suddenly increase?

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:25.170
Well, the way that our
muscles are organized

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:27.590
is such that we have muscles

00:37:27.590 --> 00:37:30.030
that are antagonistic to one another.

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:33.070
So, our quadriceps and our hamstrings

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:36.730
work in sort of a push-pull
fashion, if you will.

00:37:36.730 --> 00:37:38.540
They can antagonize one another,

00:37:38.540 --> 00:37:42.190
so when you move your
heel towards your glutes,

00:37:42.190 --> 00:37:43.760
you are using your hamstring,

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:45.580
the hamstring obviously
also does other things

00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:46.890
related to hip movement.

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:49.860
And when you lift your knee

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:53.120
or when you extend your foot

00:37:53.120 --> 00:37:55.000
and contract your quadriceps,

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:58.340
you are essentially
relaxing the hamstrings.

00:37:58.340 --> 00:37:59.500
Now, of course, most movements

00:37:59.500 --> 00:38:02.770
involve both quadricep and
hamstring in synchrony.

00:38:02.770 --> 00:38:04.950
And that synchrony is
really an elegant one,

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:06.470
but here we're more or less isolating

00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:07.760
the quadriceps from the hamstrings,

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:09.930
at least to the extent
that it can leverage

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:12.160
these spindle stretch mechanisms.

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:15.540
So, what happens is when you
contract your quadriceps hard,

00:38:15.540 --> 00:38:20.540
you are relaxing or
releasing some of the stretch

00:38:20.890 --> 00:38:24.470
that's occurring in those
intrafusal spindle sensory fibers

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:25.860
going into your spinal cord.

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:27.533
And as a consequence,

00:38:28.510 --> 00:38:33.470
you're able then to stretch
your hamstrings further,

00:38:33.470 --> 00:38:37.350
or we can be more accurate and
say that your range of motion

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:39.750
about the hamstring and its related joints

00:38:39.750 --> 00:38:44.010
is greater when you aren't
engaging that spindle reflex,

00:38:44.010 --> 00:38:46.440
which would cause the
hamstrings to contract.

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:48.800
Okay, so if you are somebody
who has tight hamstrings,

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:51.020
there could be a variety
of reasons for that,

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:54.600
but part of the reason
is likely to be neural

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:57.630
and you can release that
neural spindle reflex

00:38:57.630 --> 00:39:01.490
by contracting the opposite
antagonistic muscle,

00:39:01.490 --> 00:39:03.300
which in this case is the quadriceps.

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:05.690
The same thing is true
and can be leveraged

00:39:05.690 --> 00:39:07.040
for stretching other muscles.

00:39:07.040 --> 00:39:08.820
So, for instance, if you're
going to do a tricep stretch,

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:11.020
the typical kind of overhead
where you grab your elbow

00:39:11.020 --> 00:39:13.670
and move it toward the
midline of your body

00:39:13.670 --> 00:39:16.400
with using your opposite hand.

00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:17.710
Well, you can do that,

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:21.110
and then I would suggest
trying to flex your bicep,

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:23.730
contract your bicep that
is, while doing that.

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:26.610
And for most people
you'll notice a increase

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:28.390
in the tricep range of motion

00:39:28.390 --> 00:39:30.990
or ability to kind of lean into,

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:35.020
or to relax into, or to push that stretch,

00:39:35.020 --> 00:39:36.820
excuse me, a little bit further.

00:39:36.820 --> 00:39:38.950
Now, for you physios out there

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:42.030
and for those of you that have
backgrounds in kinesiology,

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:42.863
I want to acknowledge,

00:39:42.863 --> 00:39:44.210
of course, there are other mechanisms

00:39:44.210 --> 00:39:45.760
that are coming into play.

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:47.860
There are actually neural connections

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:49.320
within the joints themselves

00:39:49.320 --> 00:39:51.160
that are providing
proprioceptive feedback,

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:52.160
et cetera, et cetera.

00:39:52.160 --> 00:39:54.380
But this is simply to illustrate

00:39:54.380 --> 00:39:57.250
that part of our range of motion

00:39:57.250 --> 00:39:59.940
is determined by these spindle mechanisms

00:39:59.940 --> 00:40:03.080
that I spent some time
focusing on earlier.

00:40:03.080 --> 00:40:05.830
And indeed, this approach can be leveraged

00:40:05.830 --> 00:40:09.370
toward creating increased
limb range of motion,

00:40:09.370 --> 00:40:12.010
not just for the hamstrings,
but for your quadriceps.

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:14.650
So, for instance, if you
have tight quadriceps,

00:40:14.650 --> 00:40:15.720
you can do the opposite.

00:40:15.720 --> 00:40:19.610
You can contract your
hamstring very intensely

00:40:19.610 --> 00:40:23.080
for let's say 10 seconds, or
20 seconds, or 30 seconds.

00:40:23.080 --> 00:40:24.600
So, that would take some conscious effort

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:28.560
of bringing your heel
up towards your glutes.

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:29.580
You could do that in a way

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:31.640
that you're really trying to
contract those muscles hard,

00:40:31.640 --> 00:40:32.900
you'd have to use some deliberate

00:40:32.900 --> 00:40:34.190
hamstring activation there,

00:40:34.190 --> 00:40:36.097
meaning you have to use
those upper motor neurons

00:40:36.097 --> 00:40:39.990
and the other aspects of your
upper brain power, as it were,

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:42.840
to try and really contract your hamstrings

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:46.670
as intensely as possible,
then you would relax that,

00:40:46.670 --> 00:40:49.040
and then you would do your
quadricep stretch again.

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:51.730
And if you did a pre-hamstring contraction

00:40:51.730 --> 00:40:54.680
measurement of your quadricep flexibility,

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:58.040
and then you did a
post-hamstring contraction

00:40:58.040 --> 00:41:00.920
measure of your quadricep flexibility,

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:01.910
almost certainly you would find

00:41:01.910 --> 00:41:03.930
that that flexibility had increased.

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:06.910
Now, of course, the muscle
really didn't change much,

00:41:06.910 --> 00:41:08.590
the tendons didn't change much.

00:41:08.590 --> 00:41:10.930
What changed was the
patterns of neural activation

00:41:10.930 --> 00:41:13.940
that were restricting you
from in the first case

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:16.800
stretching your hamstring or having a...

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:18.300
To be more accurate we should say

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:22.260
having a certain range of
motion about the hamstring

00:41:22.260 --> 00:41:23.360
and its related joints

00:41:24.630 --> 00:41:26.820
and those brake mechanisms were removed.

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:28.570
And, of course, then when
you contract your hamstring,

00:41:28.570 --> 00:41:30.450
you're removing some of the neural brakes,

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:32.600
the spindle acting as a brake

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:37.220
and inhibiting that
quadricep range of motion.

00:41:37.220 --> 00:41:38.697
Okay, so you can imagine this,

00:41:38.697 --> 00:41:40.070
and in fact you can apply this

00:41:40.070 --> 00:41:41.800
for any number of different muscles,

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:44.030
the larger muscles and the sort of biceps,

00:41:44.030 --> 00:41:47.300
triceps, and hamstrings, quadriceps

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:49.167
that are sort of the simplest
place to think about this

00:41:49.167 --> 00:41:50.380
and to apply it.

00:41:50.380 --> 00:41:53.020
But in theory and, indeed, in practice,

00:41:53.020 --> 00:41:55.493
it really works for all
the various muscle groups,

00:41:55.493 --> 00:41:57.350
it's just sometimes harder to access

00:41:57.350 --> 00:42:00.200
these so-called
antagonistic muscle groups.

00:42:00.200 --> 00:42:04.120
Now, we should take a
moment and just discuss

00:42:04.120 --> 00:42:06.490
what actually happens
as we get more flexible

00:42:06.490 --> 00:42:07.580
in the short-term and long-term.

00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:09.660
I just mentioned what
happens in the short-term,

00:42:09.660 --> 00:42:13.950
clearly those don't involve
lengthening of the muscles.

00:42:13.950 --> 00:42:16.060
It's not like the muscles
slide along the bones

00:42:16.060 --> 00:42:18.150
or that the tendons really stretch out

00:42:18.150 --> 00:42:22.920
that much more than they had
prior to that kind of exercise.

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:26.450
But it is the case that if people stretch

00:42:26.450 --> 00:42:30.780
consistently over a given
period of several weeks or more

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:33.370
that there are changes in the muscles.

00:42:33.370 --> 00:42:35.660
This gets a little bit tricky
in terms of nomenclature,

00:42:35.660 --> 00:42:36.990
and I just want to highlight that

00:42:36.990 --> 00:42:39.470
because I think that a number of people

00:42:39.470 --> 00:42:42.440
get frustrated and confused,

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:46.390
in fact, when we talk about
muscles getting longer.

00:42:46.390 --> 00:42:48.860
You know, that the whole concept
of a muscle getting longer

00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:50.810
isn't really in keeping with reality,

00:42:50.810 --> 00:42:53.260
but there are elements within the muscles

00:42:53.260 --> 00:42:55.520
that can change their confirmation.

00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:57.340
So, to get a little bit detailed here,

00:42:57.340 --> 00:42:58.650
and we won't spend too much time on this,

00:42:58.650 --> 00:42:59.790
but I just want to acknowledge this

00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:00.750
for those of you that are interested

00:43:00.750 --> 00:43:02.090
in neuromuscular physiology

00:43:02.090 --> 00:43:04.000
and how it relates to flexibility.

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:05.900
You know, you have your muscle fibers

00:43:05.900 --> 00:43:08.570
and then you have your
so-called myofibrils.

00:43:08.570 --> 00:43:12.100
So, you can imagine
kind of a single fiber,

00:43:12.100 --> 00:43:13.620
that fiber, of course, will get input

00:43:13.620 --> 00:43:14.960
from those motor neurons.

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:17.390
And then within those fibers,

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:18.880
you have what are called sarcomeres.

00:43:18.880 --> 00:43:20.370
And you can kind of think about sarcomeres

00:43:20.370 --> 00:43:22.610
as little segments, kind of
like the segments of bamboo.

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:25.370
If you ever look at bamboo,
it's not just one big stalk,

00:43:25.370 --> 00:43:28.550
it's got those little
outpouching along the way

00:43:28.550 --> 00:43:31.430
that going to break up

00:43:31.430 --> 00:43:34.580
the what would be just
one big stalk of bamboo

00:43:34.580 --> 00:43:37.110
into different segments,
but they're all connected.

00:43:37.110 --> 00:43:40.570
The sarcomeres are somewhat like that.

00:43:40.570 --> 00:43:42.010
And within the sarcomeres

00:43:42.010 --> 00:43:43.470
you have a couple of different components.

00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:46.830
One thing is called myosin,
which is like a thick layer,

00:43:46.830 --> 00:43:48.200
and then the other is actin.

00:43:48.200 --> 00:43:50.970
And those are interdigitated, as we say,

00:43:50.970 --> 00:43:52.420
they're kind of connected to one another,

00:43:52.420 --> 00:43:54.210
kind of like if you were
put your fingers together

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:55.790
from your two hands.

00:43:55.790 --> 00:43:57.120
If you put your fingers

00:43:57.120 --> 00:43:59.140
in between one another,
that's interdigitated,

00:43:59.140 --> 00:44:03.010
literally interdigitated in
this case, so pun intended.

00:44:03.010 --> 00:44:06.780
And that myosin and actin

00:44:06.780 --> 00:44:08.610
kind of move relative to one another

00:44:08.610 --> 00:44:10.210
and they have a lot to
do with your ability

00:44:10.210 --> 00:44:11.640
to contract muscles.

00:44:11.640 --> 00:44:14.590
When we stretch muscles,

00:44:14.590 --> 00:44:16.460
when we go through a stretching practice,

00:44:16.460 --> 00:44:17.940
there are a number of things that change,

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:21.580
some neural, some related
directly to connective tissue,

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:23.710
but also it appears from really nice work

00:44:23.710 --> 00:44:26.120
mainly done from McGill University,

00:44:26.120 --> 00:44:27.590
I'll provide a link to a
couple of these studies

00:44:27.590 --> 00:44:30.190
if you want to dig in there more deeply,

00:44:30.190 --> 00:44:32.740
that change the confirmation,

00:44:32.740 --> 00:44:35.760
the relative size and spacing
of some of these things

00:44:35.760 --> 00:44:38.730
like sarcomeres and the
way that myosin and actin

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:40.430
kind of work together.

00:44:40.430 --> 00:44:44.400
But we don't want to think
of muscles as lengthening,

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:48.490
we can, however, think about
the resting state of a muscle

00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:51.580
being slightly different
or, indeed, very different

00:44:51.580 --> 00:44:54.030
than the resting state of a muscle

00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:55.670
of somebody or of a limb

00:44:55.670 --> 00:44:58.650
that has not undergone
regular flexibility training.

00:44:58.650 --> 00:45:00.520
So, that's as much time
as I want to spend on that

00:45:00.520 --> 00:45:01.900
because we could spend an entire hour

00:45:01.900 --> 00:45:03.850
getting right down into the details,

00:45:03.850 --> 00:45:05.540
but I do want to emphasize, however,

00:45:05.540 --> 00:45:07.890
that muscles have different
parts, they have fibers,

00:45:07.890 --> 00:45:11.560
they have sarcomeres, they
have myosin, they have actin.

00:45:11.560 --> 00:45:13.900
But the idea of making our muscles longer,

00:45:13.900 --> 00:45:15.870
that reflects a number of processes

00:45:15.870 --> 00:45:19.910
that occur basically within
an existing muscle length.

00:45:19.910 --> 00:45:21.760
The length of our muscle bellies

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:24.950
and where our insertions are relative

00:45:24.950 --> 00:45:26.660
to our connective tissue in our limbs

00:45:26.660 --> 00:45:29.540
is genetically determined, right?

00:45:29.540 --> 00:45:30.840
Some people have, for instance,

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:34.070
a bicep that goes all the way
from the crook of their elbow

00:45:34.070 --> 00:45:36.370
up to their shoulder, right?

00:45:36.370 --> 00:45:39.040
And some people can if
they were to put their arm

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:41.910
in a 90 degree angle could
put two or three fingers

00:45:41.910 --> 00:45:44.360
between their bicep and their elbow.

00:45:44.360 --> 00:45:48.370
They have, we can say, a shorter
bicep, relatively shorter.

00:45:48.370 --> 00:45:49.600
Now, the reason I mentioned

00:45:49.600 --> 00:45:52.130
these highly detailed cellular mechanisms

00:45:52.130 --> 00:45:56.330
is because as we start to
embark on different protocols

00:45:56.330 --> 00:45:59.257
for using stretching
to increase flexibility

00:45:59.257 --> 00:46:00.970
and range of motion,

00:46:00.970 --> 00:46:04.960
we need to ask ourselves what
is preventing our ability

00:46:04.960 --> 00:46:06.700
to extend range of motion?

00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:08.100
Is it the spindle, right?

00:46:08.100 --> 00:46:10.930
Is it because the muscle
is stretching too much?

00:46:10.930 --> 00:46:12.577
Oftentimes, it can be because of that

00:46:12.577 --> 00:46:14.590
and/or because of a sense of pain

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:18.870
or simply a sense that the
muscle is not in a position

00:46:18.870 --> 00:46:20.260
that it's been in before

00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:23.390
that's unrelated to pain
or to spindle activation.

00:46:23.390 --> 00:46:27.310
And oftentimes, it can be related directly

00:46:27.310 --> 00:46:31.340
to these changes in the
confirmation of myosin and actin,

00:46:31.340 --> 00:46:34.120
and within the context of the sarcomeres.

00:46:34.120 --> 00:46:35.840
Now, of course, you can't peer into

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:38.000
or sense your individual sarcomeres,

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:41.680
however, you do have neurons
that innovate these areas

00:46:41.680 --> 00:46:43.440
and that send that sensory information

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:46.010
back into the spinal cord and
up to your brain to interpret.

00:46:46.010 --> 00:46:48.270
So, you'll find that as we move along,

00:46:48.270 --> 00:46:50.950
there are specific
adjustments that you can make

00:46:50.950 --> 00:46:52.770
at both the macro level,

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:55.830
meaning how much movement to insert

00:46:55.830 --> 00:46:56.880
into your stretching, right?

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:57.720
Is it going to be a static,

00:46:57.720 --> 00:47:00.070
or a dynamic, or even a ballistic stretch?

00:47:00.070 --> 00:47:03.260
Or, for instance, at the micro level,

00:47:03.260 --> 00:47:06.040
that even just a slight sub-millimeter

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:10.970
or millimeter increase in the
stretching of a given muscle

00:47:10.970 --> 00:47:12.750
and it related tissues

00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:16.190
can translate into an increased
range of motion performance.

00:47:16.190 --> 00:47:17.540
As a quick but relevant aside,

00:47:17.540 --> 00:47:19.660
I thought I'd share with
you something useful

00:47:19.660 --> 00:47:24.280
that's also grounded in this
notion of antagonistic muscles.

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:26.910
So, for those of you that
do resistance training,

00:47:26.910 --> 00:47:28.230
whether or not it's with body weight,

00:47:28.230 --> 00:47:31.670
or with physical weights,
or machines, what have you,

00:47:31.670 --> 00:47:32.980
you may have found that if you,

00:47:32.980 --> 00:47:35.770
let's say, were to do three
sets of a pushing exercise,

00:47:35.770 --> 00:47:37.800
so this could be pushups,

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:40.220
this could be bench presses,
this could be shoulder presses,

00:47:40.220 --> 00:47:41.810
something of that sort.

00:47:41.810 --> 00:47:45.100
And then, later in the
workout you were to do,

00:47:45.100 --> 00:47:48.110
let's say, machine
pull-downs, or pull-ups,

00:47:48.110 --> 00:47:51.240
or chin-ups of some sort,
so a pulling exercise.

00:47:51.240 --> 00:47:52.350
Typically, what you would find

00:47:52.350 --> 00:47:55.420
is if you were to do what's
often called straight sets,

00:47:55.420 --> 00:47:57.520
so you would do three sets of pushups,

00:47:57.520 --> 00:48:00.580
let's say, with two
minutes of rest in between

00:48:00.580 --> 00:48:01.700
that you might be able to get

00:48:01.700 --> 00:48:03.980
a certain number of
repetitions on the first set.

00:48:03.980 --> 00:48:05.800
Let's just for sake of example,

00:48:05.800 --> 00:48:09.100
let's say you can get 10
repetitions on the first set,

00:48:09.100 --> 00:48:12.680
and then you get eight
repetitions on the second set,

00:48:12.680 --> 00:48:14.650
and then you get six
repetitions on the third set

00:48:14.650 --> 00:48:16.440
with two minutes in between,

00:48:16.440 --> 00:48:18.950
and then you would move on at some point

00:48:18.950 --> 00:48:20.630
to your pulling exercises.

00:48:20.630 --> 00:48:22.910
And similarly, let's say
you were doing chin-ups

00:48:22.910 --> 00:48:25.250
or pull-downs and you
would get 10 repetitions,

00:48:25.250 --> 00:48:26.083
rest two minutes,

00:48:26.083 --> 00:48:28.790
eight repetitions, rest two
minutes, and six repetitions.

00:48:28.790 --> 00:48:30.060
Okay, fine.

00:48:30.060 --> 00:48:33.190
Well, typically what people discover

00:48:33.190 --> 00:48:34.910
is that if they interleave

00:48:34.910 --> 00:48:37.090
their pushing and pulling exercises,

00:48:37.090 --> 00:48:38.790
provided they do that for muscles

00:48:38.790 --> 00:48:40.880
that are antagonistic to one another.

00:48:40.880 --> 00:48:43.860
So, in this case, pushing with the chest,

00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:46.290
shoulders, and triceps
for the pushing exercises,

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:48.480
and pulling with the back and biceps,

00:48:48.480 --> 00:48:50.610
and, of course, there are
other muscles involved as well.

00:48:50.610 --> 00:48:51.980
But because those muscle groups

00:48:51.980 --> 00:48:54.810
are at least in part
antagonistic to one another,

00:48:54.810 --> 00:48:57.090
what people often find
is that if they were

00:48:57.090 --> 00:49:00.730
to, say, do their pushing
set, get 10 repetitions,

00:49:00.730 --> 00:49:04.930
then move to a pulling set

00:49:04.930 --> 00:49:09.320
after just say 60 seconds
and perform that pulling set,

00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:13.050
then go back to the pushing set,

00:49:13.050 --> 00:49:14.630
then go back to a pulling set,

00:49:14.630 --> 00:49:16.180
push, pull, push, pull,

00:49:16.180 --> 00:49:18.070
in other words, interleaving their sets.

00:49:18.070 --> 00:49:21.520
Even if they were to maintain
the same amount of rest

00:49:21.520 --> 00:49:24.423
between sets of pushing
and sets of pulling,

00:49:25.330 --> 00:49:28.780
what they discover often is that the drop

00:49:28.780 --> 00:49:31.290
in the number of repetitions that they get

00:49:31.290 --> 00:49:32.410
is somewhat offset.

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:34.870
So, rather than get 10, 8, 6,

00:49:34.870 --> 00:49:36.500
as it were with the straight sets,

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:39.890
it will be 10, 9, 8.

00:49:39.890 --> 00:49:43.790
So, what this means is
not that you're increasing

00:49:43.790 --> 00:49:45.970
the total rest time to
four minutes between sets

00:49:45.970 --> 00:49:48.840
because then, of course,
it wouldn't be equivalent,

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:52.040
but rather that while maintaining
the same amount of rest

00:49:52.040 --> 00:49:55.470
between sets for this same muscle group,

00:49:55.470 --> 00:49:58.150
by going from push, pull, push, pull

00:49:58.150 --> 00:50:00.370
of antagonistic muscles,

00:50:00.370 --> 00:50:03.960
you're able to have improved performance.

00:50:03.960 --> 00:50:06.130
And the reason for that
has everything to do

00:50:06.130 --> 00:50:08.090
with what we were describing before,

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:12.030
which is that typically if
you were to do push set,

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:15.000
rest, push set, rest, push set, rest.

00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:17.460
Well, in between those sets

00:50:17.460 --> 00:50:20.540
and, in fact, actually
during those sets of pushing,

00:50:20.540 --> 00:50:24.480
the pulling muscles that would
be involved in the chin-ups

00:50:24.480 --> 00:50:27.750
or pull-downs, et cetera,
are actually relaxing,

00:50:27.750 --> 00:50:29.970
or at least are being
released of some tension,

00:50:29.970 --> 00:50:34.240
including the activation of the
spindles among other things.

00:50:34.240 --> 00:50:36.250
So, that's a long-winded way of saying

00:50:36.250 --> 00:50:40.300
that interleaving push and
pull of antagonistic sets

00:50:40.300 --> 00:50:42.610
can leverage some of
the same neural circuits

00:50:42.610 --> 00:50:43.760
that we're talking about leveraging

00:50:43.760 --> 00:50:45.670
for sake of increasing flexibility.

00:50:45.670 --> 00:50:49.190
Now, I offer this to you
as a tool that you can try.

00:50:49.190 --> 00:50:51.020
One of the challenges
with using this tool,

00:50:51.020 --> 00:50:54.310
however, is that you often
have to occupy multiple sites

00:50:54.310 --> 00:50:55.300
within the gym.

00:50:55.300 --> 00:50:57.060
You know, if you're doing this at home

00:50:57.060 --> 00:50:58.960
and you have your own
gym, that's one thing.

00:50:58.960 --> 00:50:59.860
If you're doing this in a gym

00:50:59.860 --> 00:51:01.620
where you have multiple
pieces of equipment,

00:51:01.620 --> 00:51:02.940
well, then you become that person

00:51:02.940 --> 00:51:05.390
who has essentially taken
over some small corner,

00:51:05.390 --> 00:51:08.370
or multiple corners, or
machines within the gym.

00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:10.120
And oftentimes, you'll
find that you'll walk back

00:51:10.120 --> 00:51:11.510
to a machine or you'll walk back

00:51:11.510 --> 00:51:13.590
to a given resistance exercise

00:51:13.590 --> 00:51:14.827
and someone has now taken it over

00:51:14.827 --> 00:51:16.210
and the whole thing could be thrown off.

00:51:16.210 --> 00:51:18.750
So, it takes a little bit of orchestrating

00:51:18.750 --> 00:51:20.270
in order to do properly.

00:51:20.270 --> 00:51:22.080
But in general, what people find

00:51:22.080 --> 00:51:25.110
is that this can allow you to
enhance performance overall

00:51:25.110 --> 00:51:26.760
of these individual movements,

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:30.960
again, while maintaining
the same amount of rest.

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:32.970
And even if you choose not to do this,

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:36.930
I encourage you to pay
attention to this as a concept

00:51:36.930 --> 00:51:39.310
because, again, it's leveraging this idea

00:51:39.310 --> 00:51:42.340
of antagonistic muscles,
flexors and extensors,

00:51:42.340 --> 00:51:44.670
antagonistic neural relationships

00:51:44.670 --> 00:51:46.680
between the spinal cord mechanisms

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:48.890
that control one set of muscles

00:51:48.890 --> 00:51:50.180
and activating those muscles,

00:51:50.180 --> 00:51:53.990
allowing the opposite
antagonistic muscle to relax

00:51:53.990 --> 00:51:57.610
and therefore to perform
better on its next set.

00:51:57.610 --> 00:51:59.160
So, now I'd like to shift to the question

00:51:59.160 --> 00:52:01.890
of what types of stretching
can and should we do

00:52:01.890 --> 00:52:04.380
to increase limb range of motion?

00:52:04.380 --> 00:52:05.520
If our goal is to do that

00:52:05.520 --> 00:52:07.120
in the most efficient way possible

00:52:07.120 --> 00:52:08.170
'cause I realize that most people

00:52:08.170 --> 00:52:10.400
don't have endless amounts of time

00:52:10.400 --> 00:52:12.250
to dedicate to a stretching practice.

00:52:13.310 --> 00:52:14.940
And even for those of us that do,

00:52:14.940 --> 00:52:18.080
I'm sure that you want
to get the most outcome

00:52:18.080 --> 00:52:19.750
for a given effort.

00:52:19.750 --> 00:52:21.640
And what are the modes of stretching

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:24.107
that are going to allow us
to increase our flexibility

00:52:24.107 --> 00:52:27.120
and limb range of motion most safely?

00:52:27.120 --> 00:52:29.100
Now, there are a number of
different types of stretching

00:52:29.100 --> 00:52:31.050
or methods of stretching.

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:36.000
Broadly defined, we can
describe these as dynamic,

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:40.680
ballistic, static, and
what's called PNF stretching.

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:45.150
PNF stands for Proprioceptive
Neuromuscular Facilitation,

00:52:45.150 --> 00:52:48.500
and it involves and leverages
many of the mechanisms

00:52:48.500 --> 00:52:50.120
that I described to you earlier.

00:52:50.120 --> 00:52:51.140
The first two that I mentioned,

00:52:51.140 --> 00:52:52.750
dynamic and ballistic stretching,

00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:55.950
both involves some degree of momentum,

00:52:55.950 --> 00:52:56.980
and can be distinguished

00:52:56.980 --> 00:52:59.740
from static and PNF-type stretching.

00:52:59.740 --> 00:53:02.070
Now, to distinguish dynamic stretching

00:53:02.070 --> 00:53:03.427
from ballistic stretching,

00:53:03.427 --> 00:53:06.450
I'd like to focus on
this element of momentum.

00:53:06.450 --> 00:53:11.363
Both involve moving a limb
through a given range of motion,

00:53:12.490 --> 00:53:14.730
in dynamic stretching, however,

00:53:14.730 --> 00:53:17.430
it tends to be more controlled,
less use of momentum,

00:53:17.430 --> 00:53:20.000
especially towards the
end range of motion.

00:53:20.000 --> 00:53:22.120
Whereas in ballistic stretching,

00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:25.580
there tends to be a bit
more swinging of the limb

00:53:25.580 --> 00:53:27.310
or use of momentum.

00:53:27.310 --> 00:53:29.440
So, I invite you to visualize what dynamic

00:53:29.440 --> 00:53:31.580
and ballistic stretching
might look like in your mind,

00:53:31.580 --> 00:53:33.880
you can even try it if it's
safe for you to try it.

00:53:33.880 --> 00:53:36.830
You know, you could imagine
swinging your arm up overhead

00:53:36.830 --> 00:53:38.340
as much as possible and bringing it down.

00:53:38.340 --> 00:53:39.530
I'm doing this because I'm seated,

00:53:39.530 --> 00:53:41.480
it's kind of a ridiculous
movement to do while seated

00:53:41.480 --> 00:53:42.880
or perhaps at all.

00:53:42.880 --> 00:53:44.160
But for instance,

00:53:44.160 --> 00:53:46.640
you can see dynamic and
ballistic stretching.

00:53:46.640 --> 00:53:49.240
Anytime someone, for instance,
is holding onto something

00:53:49.240 --> 00:53:50.990
with one arm or maybe not holding on

00:53:50.990 --> 00:53:53.690
and swinging out their foot,

00:53:53.690 --> 00:53:57.710
so essentially getting
movement about the hip joint.

00:53:57.710 --> 00:53:59.680
And you'll notice that
some people raise it up,

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:01.270
and pause it, and bring it down,

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:03.840
that's one form of dynamic stretching.

00:54:03.840 --> 00:54:07.220
Whereas others will swing it up

00:54:07.220 --> 00:54:10.310
and sort of let it carry
itself a bit further

00:54:10.310 --> 00:54:12.020
due to the momentum at
the top of the movement,

00:54:12.020 --> 00:54:13.400
and then just let it drop back down

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:14.980
or maybe even control the descent.

00:54:14.980 --> 00:54:18.670
There is an enormous range
of parameter space here

00:54:18.670 --> 00:54:20.660
or variables that one could imagine,

00:54:20.660 --> 00:54:22.150
and there's just simply no way

00:54:22.150 --> 00:54:23.340
that we could subdivide all those.

00:54:23.340 --> 00:54:25.500
But again, dynamic and
ballistic stretching

00:54:25.500 --> 00:54:27.680
both involve movement,

00:54:27.680 --> 00:54:29.270
so we have to generate some force

00:54:29.270 --> 00:54:31.500
in order to create that movement.

00:54:31.500 --> 00:54:33.657
Ballistic stretching
involving a bit more momentum

00:54:33.657 --> 00:54:35.460
or sometimes a lot more momentum,

00:54:35.460 --> 00:54:38.090
especially at the end range of of motion.

00:54:38.090 --> 00:54:40.150
Now, both of those are highly distinct

00:54:40.150 --> 00:54:41.420
from static stretching,

00:54:41.420 --> 00:54:44.250
which involves holding
the end range of motion,

00:54:44.250 --> 00:54:47.040
so minimizing the amount
of momentum that's used.

00:54:47.040 --> 00:54:48.870
So, to stay with the simple example

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:50.900
that we are all now familiar with

00:54:50.900 --> 00:54:52.403
from our earlier discussion,

00:54:53.430 --> 00:54:55.440
slowly bending over at the waist

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:57.460
and trying to touch your toes

00:54:57.460 --> 00:54:59.350
or putting your hands to the floor

00:54:59.350 --> 00:55:01.840
and then holding that end position

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:05.290
before coming up in a
slow and controlled way,

00:55:05.290 --> 00:55:09.440
such that you reduce the
amount of momentum to near zero

00:55:10.810 --> 00:55:13.290
would be one example of static stretching.

00:55:13.290 --> 00:55:15.590
Static stretching can
be further subdivided

00:55:15.590 --> 00:55:17.670
into active or passive.

00:55:17.670 --> 00:55:19.520
Right, there are different names

00:55:19.520 --> 00:55:21.380
for these kinds of approaches.

00:55:21.380 --> 00:55:23.030
You can hear about the Anderson approach

00:55:23.030 --> 00:55:24.080
or the Jander approach,

00:55:24.080 --> 00:55:26.080
you can look these sorts
of things up online.

00:55:26.080 --> 00:55:28.150
And, again, people tend to
name things after themselves,

00:55:28.150 --> 00:55:29.800
so some of these are proprietary

00:55:29.800 --> 00:55:32.420
related to specific programs,
I'm not focusing on those.

00:55:32.420 --> 00:55:34.650
Others come to be named
after the physiologists

00:55:34.650 --> 00:55:38.200
or the practitioners that
initially popularize them.

00:55:38.200 --> 00:55:39.560
As is always the case,

00:55:39.560 --> 00:55:41.100
there's always a naming and renaming

00:55:41.100 --> 00:55:43.570
and claiming of territory
with these things.

00:55:43.570 --> 00:55:45.510
For the time being, I'd
like to just emphasize

00:55:45.510 --> 00:55:48.370
that static stretching can be both active,

00:55:48.370 --> 00:55:51.790
where there's a dedicated effort

00:55:51.790 --> 00:55:54.860
on the part of the stretcher, you,

00:55:54.860 --> 00:55:59.410
to put force behind the
hold to kind of extend

00:55:59.410 --> 00:56:02.030
or literally to extend
the range of motion.

00:56:02.030 --> 00:56:05.810
And then, there's also
passive static stretching

00:56:05.810 --> 00:56:07.850
in which it's more of a relaxation

00:56:07.850 --> 00:56:09.950
into a further range of motion,

00:56:09.950 --> 00:56:11.420
and that can be a subtle distinction.

00:56:11.420 --> 00:56:13.730
And there are other ways in
which we can further distinguish

00:56:13.730 --> 00:56:15.950
active and passive static stretching.

00:56:15.950 --> 00:56:17.210
But nonetheless, static stretching

00:56:17.210 --> 00:56:21.410
involves both those types of
elements, active and passive,

00:56:21.410 --> 00:56:24.280
but is really about eliminating momentum.

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:26.637
And then, there's the PNF,

00:56:26.637 --> 00:56:28.700
the Proprioceptive
Neuromuscular Facilitation.

00:56:28.700 --> 00:56:31.800
And proprioception has
several different meanings

00:56:31.800 --> 00:56:34.440
in the context of
neuroscience and physiology.

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:36.820
To just keep it really simple for today,

00:56:36.820 --> 00:56:40.150
proprioception involves both
a knowledge and understanding

00:56:40.150 --> 00:56:44.510
of where our limbs are in
space and relative to our body.

00:56:44.510 --> 00:56:45.930
Typically, relative to the midline,

00:56:45.930 --> 00:56:48.060
so the brain is often trying to figure out

00:56:48.060 --> 00:56:50.750
where are our limbs
relative to our midline

00:56:50.750 --> 00:56:52.520
down the center of our body.

00:56:52.520 --> 00:56:54.910
And we know where our limbs are

00:56:54.910 --> 00:56:57.130
based on so-called
proprioceptive feedback,

00:56:57.130 --> 00:57:00.640
so that's feedback that
comes from sensory neurons.

00:57:00.640 --> 00:57:02.550
Right, now you know what sensory neurons

00:57:02.550 --> 00:57:05.480
that are essentially monitoring

00:57:05.480 --> 00:57:08.630
or responding to events within the joints,

00:57:08.630 --> 00:57:11.320
the connective tissue, and the muscles,

00:57:11.320 --> 00:57:14.970
and within the deep
components of the muscles

00:57:14.970 --> 00:57:16.130
like the spindle reflex,

00:57:16.130 --> 00:57:18.490
and within the tendons like the GTO,

00:57:18.490 --> 00:57:20.100
the Golgi tendon organ.

00:57:20.100 --> 00:57:23.340
So, PNF-type stretching

00:57:23.340 --> 00:57:28.240
leverages these sorts of
mechanisms, these neural circuits,

00:57:28.240 --> 00:57:32.150
by way of, for instance,
you would lie on your back

00:57:32.150 --> 00:57:35.350
and if your goal is to increase
your hamstring flexibility

00:57:35.350 --> 00:57:37.620
and the flexibility and range of motion

00:57:37.620 --> 00:57:40.210
of other related muscle systems,

00:57:40.210 --> 00:57:42.350
you might put a strap around your ankle

00:57:42.350 --> 00:57:45.140
and pull that muscle.

00:57:45.140 --> 00:57:47.490
Or I should say, excuse
me, that limb towards you,

00:57:47.490 --> 00:57:48.550
you're not going to pull
the muscle towards you,

00:57:48.550 --> 00:57:49.933
you're going to pull that
limb your ankle towards you

00:57:49.933 --> 00:57:52.610
to try and get it sort
of back over your head.

00:57:52.610 --> 00:57:55.530
And then, progressively
relaxing into that,

00:57:55.530 --> 00:57:58.460
or maybe even putting
some additional force

00:57:58.460 --> 00:58:01.860
to push the end range of
motion and then relaxing it.

00:58:01.860 --> 00:58:04.810
And then, actually trying
to stretch that same limb

00:58:04.810 --> 00:58:08.210
or increase the limb range
of motion without the strap.

00:58:08.210 --> 00:58:11.530
Right, sometimes these are
assisted by other people.

00:58:11.530 --> 00:58:13.720
So, people will even use loads,

00:58:13.720 --> 00:58:15.700
sometimes they'll even use machines.

00:58:15.700 --> 00:58:17.720
There are a number of different apparati

00:58:17.720 --> 00:58:18.950
that have been designed for this,

00:58:18.950 --> 00:58:21.820
sometimes it'll involve
a training partner.

00:58:21.820 --> 00:58:25.170
There's a huge range of PNF protocols,

00:58:25.170 --> 00:58:28.950
and those protocols can
be done both by oneself

00:58:28.950 --> 00:58:30.900
with or without straps, with machines,

00:58:30.900 --> 00:58:34.500
with actual weights, or
with training partners.

00:58:34.500 --> 00:58:36.960
If you're interested in
the variation of exercises

00:58:36.960 --> 00:58:39.990
to, say, target your hamstrings
versus your quadriceps

00:58:39.990 --> 00:58:42.660
versus your shoulders versus
your chest muscles, et cetera,

00:58:42.660 --> 00:58:44.720
your neck muscles, and so on,

00:58:44.720 --> 00:58:47.680
there is an enormous range of information

00:58:47.680 --> 00:58:50.620
on dynamic, ballistic,
static, and PNF stretches

00:58:50.620 --> 00:58:52.630
for all the various muscle groups.

00:58:52.630 --> 00:58:55.340
And I should say there
are some excellent books

00:58:55.340 --> 00:58:56.210
on those topics,

00:58:56.210 --> 00:58:58.080
there are also some excellent videos

00:58:58.080 --> 00:58:59.900
on YouTube and elsewhere.

00:58:59.900 --> 00:59:03.330
Nowadays, it's pretty
easy to find exercises

00:59:03.330 --> 00:59:06.870
that allow you to target
specific muscle groups.

00:59:06.870 --> 00:59:09.360
Again, I encourage you to be
safe in how you approach this,

00:59:09.360 --> 00:59:11.870
and I would encourage
you also to pay attention

00:59:11.870 --> 00:59:13.560
to the information that soon follows

00:59:13.560 --> 00:59:15.970
as to what sorts of
protocols one would use

00:59:15.970 --> 00:59:17.810
to apply those exercises.

00:59:17.810 --> 00:59:20.040
But the number of exercises

00:59:20.040 --> 00:59:22.040
and the availability of those exercises

00:59:22.040 --> 00:59:23.280
for targeting different muscle groups

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:25.360
with these four different
kinds of stretching

00:59:25.360 --> 00:59:29.910
is both immense and
fortunately, thankfully,

00:59:29.910 --> 00:59:32.830
immediately accessible to
all of us often at zero cost.

00:59:32.830 --> 00:59:34.680
So, specific exercises

00:59:34.680 --> 00:59:36.560
to target specific muscle groups aside,

00:59:36.560 --> 00:59:37.490
we've now established

00:59:37.490 --> 00:59:40.240
that there are four major
categories of stretching,

00:59:40.240 --> 00:59:42.440
or at least those are
the four major categories

00:59:42.440 --> 00:59:43.663
I'm defining today.

00:59:44.530 --> 00:59:47.940
And we can further divide those categories

00:59:47.940 --> 00:59:51.060
into which are the ones that
are going to be most effective

00:59:51.060 --> 00:59:53.740
for increasing range of
motion in the long-term,

00:59:53.740 --> 00:59:56.200
not just in one individual session.

00:59:56.200 --> 00:59:59.380
And there have been a number
of studies exploring this.

00:59:59.380 --> 01:00:00.840
I can list out at least four,

01:00:00.840 --> 01:00:03.430
and we'll put those four
as a kind of a cluster

01:00:03.430 --> 01:00:06.090
under one heading in
the show note captions

01:00:06.090 --> 01:00:09.180
that arrive at essentially
the same answer,

01:00:09.180 --> 01:00:12.860
which is that for increasing
limb range of motion,

01:00:12.860 --> 01:00:16.190
it does appear that static type,

01:00:16.190 --> 01:00:20.340
including PNF, but static-type stretching

01:00:20.340 --> 01:00:22.030
is going to be more effective

01:00:22.030 --> 01:00:24.913
than dynamic and ballistic stretching.

01:00:25.800 --> 01:00:28.690
So, at least in my
mind, this is good news.

01:00:28.690 --> 01:00:29.850
Why is it good news to me?

01:00:29.850 --> 01:00:32.720
Well, while dynamic and
ballistic stretching

01:00:32.720 --> 01:00:36.450
can be immensely useful
for improving performance

01:00:36.450 --> 01:00:38.590
of specific movements,

01:00:38.590 --> 01:00:41.060
in particular, in the
context of particular sports

01:00:41.060 --> 01:00:45.520
like tennis, or in sprinting,
or frankly for any sport,

01:00:45.520 --> 01:00:48.540
they do carry with them
a certain amount of risk

01:00:48.540 --> 01:00:50.490
because of the use of momentum.

01:00:50.490 --> 01:00:52.610
So, you don't need to be highly trained

01:00:52.610 --> 01:00:53.710
in order to perform them.

01:00:53.710 --> 01:00:55.210
In fact, there is a place

01:00:55.210 --> 01:00:58.220
and we will describe when one
would want to apply dynamic

01:00:58.220 --> 01:00:59.920
or ballistic stretching.

01:00:59.920 --> 01:01:01.830
I'll just give away for now,

01:01:01.830 --> 01:01:03.680
I think that most physios out there

01:01:03.680 --> 01:01:04.910
and certainly the ones that I spoke to,

01:01:04.910 --> 01:01:08.970
Dr. Andy Galpin, Dr. Kelly
Starrett, and a few others

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:14.040
point to the fact that doing some safe

01:01:14.890 --> 01:01:16.440
dynamic and ballistic stretching

01:01:16.440 --> 01:01:19.930
prior to, say, a resistance
training session,

01:01:19.930 --> 01:01:23.330
or maybe even prior to a
cardiovascular training session

01:01:23.330 --> 01:01:27.540
can be useful both in terms
of range of motion effects

01:01:27.540 --> 01:01:30.570
and in terms of neural activation effects.

01:01:30.570 --> 01:01:31.940
I don't want to use the words warm-up

01:01:31.940 --> 01:01:33.830
because warming up is typically associated

01:01:33.830 --> 01:01:37.010
with increasing core body
temperature, as it should be,

01:01:37.010 --> 01:01:40.510
but for engaging the neural circuits

01:01:40.510 --> 01:01:43.220
and becoming familiarized
with the neural circuits

01:01:43.220 --> 01:01:45.160
that you're about to
use in other movements

01:01:45.160 --> 01:01:47.960
while also increasing the
range of motion of the joints

01:01:47.960 --> 01:01:48.890
involved in those movements

01:01:48.890 --> 01:01:50.470
so that you can perform them more safely

01:01:50.470 --> 01:01:51.810
and more confidently.

01:01:51.810 --> 01:01:54.060
So, I'm certainly not saying,

01:01:54.060 --> 01:01:55.370
I want to repeat, I'm certainly not saying

01:01:55.370 --> 01:01:57.510
that dynamic and ballistic
stretching are not useful,

01:01:57.510 --> 01:01:59.100
they absolutely are,

01:01:59.100 --> 01:02:01.600
but in terms of increasing
limb range of motion

01:02:01.600 --> 01:02:06.180
in the long-term of truly
becoming more flexible

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:08.423
as opposed to transiently more flexible,

01:02:10.010 --> 01:02:12.693
static stretching, which includes PNF,

01:02:14.140 --> 01:02:15.820
appears to be the best route to go.

01:02:15.820 --> 01:02:18.440
So, if your goal is to increase
your limb range of motion

01:02:18.440 --> 01:02:21.850
for a given muscle group, or
perhaps for all muscle groups,

01:02:21.850 --> 01:02:23.650
although you can imagine
that'd be pretty tough.

01:02:23.650 --> 01:02:25.330
I mean, you're not going to spend time,

01:02:25.330 --> 01:02:28.350
I could imagine, working on
your tongue muscle control

01:02:28.350 --> 01:02:31.030
or neck muscle control
and every muscle control,

01:02:31.030 --> 01:02:34.140
but most of us want to
reduce so-called tightness

01:02:34.140 --> 01:02:36.750
in air quotes and increase
limb range of motion

01:02:36.750 --> 01:02:38.530
for certain muscle groups.

01:02:38.530 --> 01:02:41.760
And it appears that
the best way to do that

01:02:41.760 --> 01:02:44.460
is going to be static
stretching of some kind,

01:02:44.460 --> 01:02:46.600
which raises the question of how often

01:02:46.600 --> 01:02:47.790
to do that static stretching

01:02:47.790 --> 01:02:51.070
and how long to hold
those static stretches.

01:02:51.070 --> 01:02:52.870
And we can also ask the question,

01:02:52.870 --> 01:02:54.650
we should ask the question,

01:02:54.650 --> 01:02:56.620
where to hold those static stretches?

01:02:56.620 --> 01:02:59.750
Is it always a good idea to
hold those static stretches

01:02:59.750 --> 01:03:04.180
at the end or the point of
maximal range of motion?

01:03:04.180 --> 01:03:05.580
We're going to address that now.

01:03:05.580 --> 01:03:07.710
There's some terrific science around this.

01:03:07.710 --> 01:03:08.870
A slightly older study,

01:03:08.870 --> 01:03:10.980
but nonetheless a powerful one

01:03:10.980 --> 01:03:12.810
because it provided a foundation

01:03:12.810 --> 01:03:14.100
for a lot of subsequent work,

01:03:14.100 --> 01:03:15.990
which basically served to just confirm

01:03:15.990 --> 01:03:19.990
the answer they got here is
a study from Bandy et al.

01:03:19.990 --> 01:03:21.580
And the title of this study

01:03:21.580 --> 01:03:24.500
is The Effect of Time and
Frequency of Static Stretching

01:03:24.500 --> 01:03:27.788
on the Flexibility of
the Hamstring Muscles.

01:03:27.788 --> 01:03:30.040
It was a study involving 93 subjects,

01:03:30.040 --> 01:03:34.860
so 61 men, 32 women ranging
an age from 21-39 years,

01:03:34.860 --> 01:03:37.250
so a pretty broad demographic

01:03:37.250 --> 01:03:39.700
who had limited hamstring
muscle flexibility,

01:03:39.700 --> 01:03:40.700
here I'm paraphrasing,

01:03:40.700 --> 01:03:43.190
and randomly assigned
to one of five groups.

01:03:43.190 --> 01:03:45.340
So, the four stretching groups

01:03:45.340 --> 01:03:48.350
stretched five days
per week for six weeks,

01:03:48.350 --> 01:03:51.550
the fifth group, which served
as a control, did not stretch.

01:03:51.550 --> 01:03:54.517
The results clearly show that quote,

01:03:54.517 --> 01:03:56.790
"The change in flexibility
appeared to be dependent

01:03:56.790 --> 01:03:58.750
on the duration and
frequency of stretching."

01:03:58.750 --> 01:03:59.583
This is great,

01:03:59.583 --> 01:04:02.650
this tells us that stretching
for a given amount of time

01:04:02.650 --> 01:04:05.000
scales with the amount
of limb range of motion

01:04:05.000 --> 01:04:06.860
improvement that one will see.

01:04:06.860 --> 01:04:09.560
There were many interesting
findings within this study,

01:04:09.560 --> 01:04:13.127
but the one that I'd like
to highlight most is quote,

01:04:13.127 --> 01:04:17.240
"The results of this study
suggest that a 30 second duration

01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:18.750
is an effective amount of time

01:04:18.750 --> 01:04:20.600
to sustain a hamstring muscle stretch

01:04:20.600 --> 01:04:23.350
in order to increase range of motion.

01:04:23.350 --> 01:04:25.500
No increase in flexibility occurred

01:04:25.500 --> 01:04:27.340
when the duration of
stretching was increased

01:04:27.340 --> 01:04:30.270
from 30 seconds to 60 seconds,

01:04:30.270 --> 01:04:32.600
or when the frequency of
stretching was increased

01:04:32.600 --> 01:04:35.090
from one to three times per day."

01:04:35.090 --> 01:04:38.310
Okay, so now we're starting
to lay down some parameters.

01:04:38.310 --> 01:04:41.510
What this study reveals and
what subsequent studies tell us,

01:04:41.510 --> 01:04:44.130
and we will get into
those subsequent studies,

01:04:44.130 --> 01:04:48.380
is that ideally one
would do static stretches

01:04:48.380 --> 01:04:51.790
that are held for 30 seconds.

01:04:51.790 --> 01:04:53.440
Perhaps more in certain instances,

01:04:53.440 --> 01:04:55.220
and I'll explain when that can be useful,

01:04:55.220 --> 01:04:59.450
but here holding those stretches
for more than 30 seconds

01:04:59.450 --> 01:05:02.630
did not turn out to be
additionally useful.

01:05:02.630 --> 01:05:04.900
So, if you're going to stretch
your quadricep, for instance,

01:05:04.900 --> 01:05:07.590
and you're going to hold that
stretch in static fashion,

01:05:07.590 --> 01:05:09.900
remember not using momentum,

01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:12.610
and you can use the mental tricks

01:05:12.610 --> 01:05:14.890
of either trying to push through the pain,

01:05:14.890 --> 01:05:17.020
which I don't recommend necessarily,

01:05:17.020 --> 01:05:19.590
I think that makes us prone to injury,

01:05:19.590 --> 01:05:21.250
or to relax into the stretch,

01:05:21.250 --> 01:05:23.510
but nonetheless providing some force

01:05:23.510 --> 01:05:26.650
typically with a hand in
order to pull your ankle back,

01:05:26.650 --> 01:05:27.880
if you're doing a quadricep stretch,

01:05:27.880 --> 01:05:29.800
some people might do this
on the edge of a sofa.

01:05:29.800 --> 01:05:31.660
Remember, there are a lot
of different exercises

01:05:31.660 --> 01:05:34.300
and ways to do this that
you can explore elsewhere.

01:05:34.300 --> 01:05:37.750
Well, holding that static
stretch for 30 seconds

01:05:37.750 --> 01:05:39.970
appears to be sufficient

01:05:39.970 --> 01:05:44.090
to stimulate an increase in
limb range of motion over time.

01:05:44.090 --> 01:05:44.990
Again, these are protocols

01:05:44.990 --> 01:05:46.400
that were used repeatedly over time,

01:05:46.400 --> 01:05:48.680
and we'll talk about
how often to repeat them

01:05:48.680 --> 01:05:50.300
in order to get maximal effect.

01:05:50.300 --> 01:05:52.950
But 30-second holds for static stretches

01:05:52.950 --> 01:05:54.860
is the number that I
think we want to focus on

01:05:54.860 --> 01:05:56.990
and that most of us are
going to want to utilize.

01:05:56.990 --> 01:06:00.900
So, now let's explore how
many sets of static stretching

01:06:00.900 --> 01:06:03.420
one ought to do in order to get

01:06:03.420 --> 01:06:06.180
a maximum range of motion improvement

01:06:06.180 --> 01:06:08.800
while not placing us into a system

01:06:08.800 --> 01:06:10.290
that's going to create injury

01:06:10.290 --> 01:06:13.910
nor a situation where we have
to be constantly stretching

01:06:13.910 --> 01:06:14.743
throughout the day

01:06:14.743 --> 01:06:17.150
because, again, most of us
don't have time to do that.

01:06:17.150 --> 01:06:19.550
This issue of sets is an important one.

01:06:19.550 --> 01:06:21.540
In the context of cardiovascular exercise,

01:06:21.540 --> 01:06:24.520
we've talked about the
data that support the fact

01:06:24.520 --> 01:06:27.730
that doing at least 150

01:06:27.730 --> 01:06:30.330
and ideally as much as
200 minutes per week

01:06:30.330 --> 01:06:32.370
of Zone 2 cardiovascular exercise

01:06:32.370 --> 01:06:34.150
is very useful for cardiovascular health

01:06:34.150 --> 01:06:35.920
and for other aspects of health.

01:06:35.920 --> 01:06:36.990
And, of course, there are other aspects

01:06:36.990 --> 01:06:38.230
of cardiovascular exercise

01:06:38.230 --> 01:06:39.683
that could be layered onto and into that

01:06:39.683 --> 01:06:41.830
that can be useful like 90 second

01:06:41.830 --> 01:06:43.380
maximal sprints, et cetera.

01:06:43.380 --> 01:06:46.150
Discussed this a lot in the
episode with Dr. Andy Galpin

01:06:46.150 --> 01:06:48.210
and on our episode about endurance.

01:06:48.210 --> 01:06:50.130
And we also talked about sets

01:06:50.130 --> 01:06:52.830
in the context of strength
and hypertrophy building,

01:06:52.830 --> 01:06:55.420
building muscle size and/or strength

01:06:55.420 --> 01:06:57.350
in the episode about that.

01:06:57.350 --> 01:07:00.200
And in particular, in the
episode with Dr. Andy Galpin,

01:07:00.200 --> 01:07:03.040
and there, we could also arrive
at some specific parameters.

01:07:03.040 --> 01:07:05.760
And it's going to vary, of
course, between individuals,

01:07:05.760 --> 01:07:07.150
depending on how hard you train,

01:07:07.150 --> 01:07:08.780
whether or not you take sets to failure,

01:07:08.780 --> 01:07:10.630
your repetition range, et cetera,

01:07:10.630 --> 01:07:13.020
but in the context of strength
and hypertrophy building,

01:07:13.020 --> 01:07:16.780
we arrived at a approximately six,

01:07:16.780 --> 01:07:19.900
maybe as many as 10 sets
per week per muscle group.

01:07:19.900 --> 01:07:21.640
Some of that work is done as direct work

01:07:21.640 --> 01:07:24.350
to a given muscle group, some
of that work is indirect.

01:07:24.350 --> 01:07:25.950
So, doing certain pulling exercise,

01:07:25.950 --> 01:07:30.950
of course, will target the
latissimus dorsi muscles,

01:07:31.010 --> 01:07:32.010
but also the biceps.

01:07:32.010 --> 01:07:34.980
So, that doesn't necessarily
mean you have to do 10 sets

01:07:34.980 --> 01:07:36.500
for the biceps and for the lats,

01:07:36.500 --> 01:07:38.360
sometimes you're getting some
indirect work, et cetera.

01:07:38.360 --> 01:07:40.340
All of that was delineated in the episode

01:07:40.340 --> 01:07:41.800
with Dr. Andy Galpin.

01:07:41.800 --> 01:07:44.340
And we arrived at those numbers of sets

01:07:44.340 --> 01:07:47.370
according to the same criteria
that we will apply here,

01:07:47.370 --> 01:07:50.320
what is the minimum number of sets

01:07:50.320 --> 01:07:54.100
both to maintain and to improve

01:07:54.100 --> 01:07:56.660
a given mode of performance?

01:07:56.660 --> 01:07:59.390
Strength and hypertrophy
or cardiovascular health,

01:07:59.390 --> 01:08:01.320
again, to either maintain or improve.

01:08:01.320 --> 01:08:04.390
And we can do the same thing for improving

01:08:04.390 --> 01:08:06.250
or maintaining range of motion

01:08:06.250 --> 01:08:07.770
because as I mentioned earlier,

01:08:07.770 --> 01:08:08.960
the data pointed to the fact

01:08:08.960 --> 01:08:11.430
that if we don't do some dedicated work

01:08:11.430 --> 01:08:13.770
to improve range of motion over time,

01:08:13.770 --> 01:08:15.970
we will lose our flexibility

01:08:15.970 --> 01:08:18.180
and limb range of motion over time

01:08:18.180 --> 01:08:19.690
just by virtue of the fact

01:08:19.690 --> 01:08:21.740
that we're not doing
anything to offset that.

01:08:21.740 --> 01:08:24.360
So, whether or not you want to maintain,

01:08:24.360 --> 01:08:29.090
reestablish, or gain limb range of motion,

01:08:29.090 --> 01:08:34.000
static stretching of holds of
30 seconds appear to be best.

01:08:34.000 --> 01:08:36.910
Now, the question is how
long should you do that?

01:08:36.910 --> 01:08:38.110
And how many sets should you do that?

01:08:38.110 --> 01:08:40.210
And how many times a
week should you do that?

01:08:40.210 --> 01:08:42.350
And to answer those questions,

01:08:42.350 --> 01:08:43.760
I'm going to turn to what I think

01:08:43.760 --> 01:08:45.470
is a really spectacular review.

01:08:45.470 --> 01:08:48.150
This was a review that was
published in the year 2018,

01:08:48.150 --> 01:08:49.340
so it's fairly recent,

01:08:49.340 --> 01:08:51.510
first author Thomas, Ewan Thomas,

01:08:51.510 --> 01:08:52.920
last author Palma.

01:08:52.920 --> 01:08:55.090
We will put a link to this
in the show note caption.

01:08:55.090 --> 01:08:56.050
The title of the paper

01:08:56.050 --> 01:08:58.430
is The Relation Between
Stretching Typology

01:08:58.430 --> 01:09:00.917
and Stretching Duration: The
Effects on Range of Motion.

01:09:00.917 --> 01:09:02.683
It's a very straightforward title.

01:09:04.160 --> 01:09:07.380
This is a review article that explored

01:09:07.380 --> 01:09:08.943
a number of different studies,

01:09:10.380 --> 01:09:12.100
had criteria for whether
or not those studies

01:09:12.100 --> 01:09:15.510
could be evaluated in the
context of the questions here,

01:09:15.510 --> 01:09:17.650
had some quality standards
and some other standards

01:09:17.650 --> 01:09:18.483
that they applied.

01:09:18.483 --> 01:09:22.440
And basically, windowed down
a large collection of studies

01:09:22.440 --> 01:09:24.900
to a remaining 23 articles

01:09:24.900 --> 01:09:27.850
that were able to be
considered quote, "Eligible

01:09:27.850 --> 01:09:30.910
and included in the quantitative
synthesis done here."

01:09:30.910 --> 01:09:35.000
So, key points from that quantification

01:09:35.000 --> 01:09:37.140
and synthesis done in this paper.

01:09:37.140 --> 01:09:40.160
First of all, and I quote,
"All stretching typologies

01:09:40.160 --> 01:09:43.260
showed range of motion improvements
over a long-term period.

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:46.950
However, the static protocols
showed significant gains

01:09:46.950 --> 01:09:48.610
with a p-value less than .05,"

01:09:48.610 --> 01:09:52.240
which means a probability
that cannot be explained

01:09:52.240 --> 01:09:54.007
by chance alone,

01:09:54.007 --> 01:09:56.520
"When compared to ballistic
or PNF protocols."

01:09:56.520 --> 01:09:58.750
So, again, that we're hearing
is that static stretching

01:09:58.750 --> 01:10:02.080
is the preferred mode for
increasing limb range of motion.

01:10:02.080 --> 01:10:04.660
Although, here they make
the additional point

01:10:04.660 --> 01:10:07.740
that static stretching
might even be superior,

01:10:07.740 --> 01:10:12.740
not just to ballistic stretching,
but also to PNF protocols.

01:10:12.940 --> 01:10:15.040
Because before, as you may recall,

01:10:15.040 --> 01:10:19.980
there was a distinction
between ballistic and dynamic,

01:10:19.980 --> 01:10:21.340
and static and PNF.

01:10:21.340 --> 01:10:24.290
And so, here it appears
again that static stretching

01:10:24.290 --> 01:10:27.270
is sort of rising to the top of the list

01:10:27.270 --> 01:10:28.620
as the optimal approach

01:10:28.620 --> 01:10:31.730
relative to all other
stretching approaches,

01:10:31.730 --> 01:10:34.923
at least in the context of
increasing limb range of motion.

01:10:36.550 --> 01:10:39.800
The authors go on to say,
"Time spent stretching per week

01:10:39.800 --> 01:10:42.740
seems fundamental to elicit
range of movement improvements

01:10:42.740 --> 01:10:45.320
when stretches are applied
for at least or more

01:10:45.320 --> 01:10:47.720
than five minutes per week."

01:10:47.720 --> 01:10:48.890
Okay, this is critical.

01:10:48.890 --> 01:10:50.940
This is not five minutes per stretch.

01:10:50.940 --> 01:10:53.130
Remember, 30 seconds per static stretch,

01:10:53.130 --> 01:10:55.490
but at least five minutes per week.

01:10:55.490 --> 01:10:58.700
Whereas the time spent stretching
within a single session

01:10:58.700 --> 01:11:00.470
does not seem to have a significant effect

01:11:00.470 --> 01:11:01.640
for range of motion gains.

01:11:01.640 --> 01:11:02.960
If this is getting confusing,

01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:05.030
I'll make sure that you soon understand

01:11:05.030 --> 01:11:08.123
exactly what we can export
from these conclusions.

01:11:09.950 --> 01:11:11.730
The data indicated that
performing stretching

01:11:11.730 --> 01:11:13.763
at least five days a week.

01:11:14.630 --> 01:11:16.660
Now, some of you may already be groaning,

01:11:16.660 --> 01:11:18.770
for at least five minutes per week.

01:11:18.770 --> 01:11:21.140
Okay, so five days per week, that's a lot,

01:11:21.140 --> 01:11:23.200
but at least five minutes per week,

01:11:23.200 --> 01:11:24.967
five minutes per week is not that much.

01:11:24.967 --> 01:11:27.400
"Using static stretching may be beneficial

01:11:27.400 --> 01:11:30.270
to promote range of motion improvements."

01:11:30.270 --> 01:11:33.040
Okay, I've read this study in detail now,

01:11:33.040 --> 01:11:35.340
they highlight, again, the
reduction in flexibility

01:11:35.340 --> 01:11:38.850
that occurs from 20-49
years of age and so on,

01:11:38.850 --> 01:11:41.130
how acute bouts of short-term stretching

01:11:41.130 --> 01:11:43.420
up to three weeks can
improve stretch tolerance.

01:11:43.420 --> 01:11:46.030
I think that's a key point
that in the short-term,

01:11:46.030 --> 01:11:47.400
the first three weeks of embarking

01:11:47.400 --> 01:11:50.010
on a stretching and flexibility program,

01:11:50.010 --> 01:11:52.340
much of the improvements
come from the short-term

01:11:52.340 --> 01:11:54.130
neural improvements that
we talked about before

01:11:54.130 --> 01:11:55.980
of inhibiting the
spindle reflex and so on.

01:11:55.980 --> 01:11:57.570
And also, a stretch tolerance,

01:11:57.570 --> 01:11:59.110
a comfort with doing the movements

01:11:59.110 --> 01:12:00.710
and maybe even a comfort in overriding

01:12:00.710 --> 01:12:01.780
some of the pain mechanisms.

01:12:01.780 --> 01:12:04.220
I'll talk a little bit more
about that in just a bit

01:12:04.220 --> 01:12:07.740
and the particular utility of yoga.

01:12:07.740 --> 01:12:09.860
Something that I don't often practice,

01:12:09.860 --> 01:12:11.380
but that after reading this article

01:12:11.380 --> 01:12:12.750
that I'll mention in a little bit,

01:12:12.750 --> 01:12:14.460
I'm considering perhaps taking up

01:12:14.460 --> 01:12:17.300
some form of yoga protocol.

01:12:17.300 --> 01:12:18.410
Now, I've already highlighted

01:12:18.410 --> 01:12:20.670
some of the key take aways from the study,

01:12:20.670 --> 01:12:25.150
namely that we need to get at
least five minutes per week

01:12:25.150 --> 01:12:27.710
of static stretching per muscle group.

01:12:27.710 --> 01:12:31.980
And based on the previous
paper that we talked about,

01:12:31.980 --> 01:12:33.570
we need to divide that five minutes

01:12:33.570 --> 01:12:36.620
into sets of 30 seconds each.

01:12:36.620 --> 01:12:38.710
And as I mentioned earlier,

01:12:38.710 --> 01:12:40.110
it doesn't seem to be the case

01:12:40.110 --> 01:12:44.130
that you can do all of that
in one day, unfortunately.

01:12:44.130 --> 01:12:46.360
It does seem important that the frequency

01:12:46.360 --> 01:12:47.560
of stretching practice

01:12:47.560 --> 01:12:50.120
distributed throughout
the week is important.

01:12:50.120 --> 01:12:52.190
So, let's talk protocols.

01:12:52.190 --> 01:12:54.280
We are now talking about
doing static stretching,

01:12:54.280 --> 01:12:56.050
so holding, so limiting momentum

01:12:56.050 --> 01:12:58.500
and holding a stretch
for 30 seconds per set.

01:12:58.500 --> 01:13:01.030
We're talking about trying to achieve

01:13:01.030 --> 01:13:04.000
five minutes per week
of those static holds,

01:13:04.000 --> 01:13:08.070
but that we can't do it all in one session

01:13:08.070 --> 01:13:10.160
because the frequency of sessions

01:13:10.160 --> 01:13:11.470
distributed throughout the week

01:13:11.470 --> 01:13:14.410
correlates with the improvements
in limb range of motion.

01:13:14.410 --> 01:13:17.670
So, what this means is that
we should probably be doing

01:13:17.670 --> 01:13:20.630
anywhere from two to four sets

01:13:20.630 --> 01:13:23.580
of 30 second static holds stretches

01:13:25.430 --> 01:13:29.660
five days per week or
some variant thereof.

01:13:29.660 --> 01:13:32.410
And I do say some variant
thereof because it turns out

01:13:32.410 --> 01:13:34.020
that even though there
was that earlier study

01:13:34.020 --> 01:13:35.180
that we talked about

01:13:35.180 --> 01:13:37.980
that holding a stretch
for more than 30 seconds,

01:13:37.980 --> 01:13:39.580
in that case 60 seconds,

01:13:39.580 --> 01:13:42.400
didn't turn out to be
additionally beneficial.

01:13:42.400 --> 01:13:45.070
It appears that if you
do hold those stretches

01:13:45.070 --> 01:13:49.080
for 60 seconds per static
stretching set, for instance,

01:13:49.080 --> 01:13:50.900
you can get away with stretching

01:13:50.900 --> 01:13:53.410
fewer days per week overall.

01:13:53.410 --> 01:13:56.950
So, in order to make
this as clear as possible

01:13:56.950 --> 01:13:59.350
'cause I do realize there
are a lot of parameters

01:13:59.350 --> 01:14:00.457
and you might be asking,

01:14:00.457 --> 01:14:01.700
"Why didn't you just make me a list

01:14:01.700 --> 01:14:03.040
of the exact things I should do?"

01:14:03.040 --> 01:14:04.170
Well, it doesn't work that way

01:14:04.170 --> 01:14:06.130
because once you understand the mechanisms

01:14:06.130 --> 01:14:08.450
and once you understand
your particular goals,

01:14:08.450 --> 01:14:11.260
this information is designed
for you to be able to construct

01:14:11.260 --> 01:14:13.130
a stretching program that is tailored

01:14:13.130 --> 01:14:14.610
to your specific goals.

01:14:14.610 --> 01:14:16.990
If I just gave you the stretching
program that I'm doing,

01:14:16.990 --> 01:14:18.540
or I should say that I'm soon to be doing

01:14:18.540 --> 01:14:19.800
'cause I'm soon to be doing one

01:14:19.800 --> 01:14:23.560
based on the research for
this particular episode.

01:14:23.560 --> 01:14:25.490
Well, that wouldn't be beneficial for you

01:14:25.490 --> 01:14:28.390
because, for instance, if you
have very flexible hamstrings,

01:14:28.390 --> 01:14:30.330
but not very flexible quadriceps,

01:14:30.330 --> 01:14:32.940
or you are somebody
who is engaged in sport

01:14:32.940 --> 01:14:34.950
or not engaged in sport,

01:14:34.950 --> 01:14:36.950
what you need to do is
going to vary somewhat.

01:14:36.950 --> 01:14:40.370
So, what would effective
stretching protocol look like?

01:14:40.370 --> 01:14:42.630
We're all trying to improve
limb range of motion

01:14:42.630 --> 01:14:45.930
for different limbs and
different muscle groups.

01:14:45.930 --> 01:14:47.240
But just by way of example,

01:14:47.240 --> 01:14:48.440
and it's because the one we've been using,

01:14:48.440 --> 01:14:51.580
let's talk about hamstrings
for the time being.

01:14:51.580 --> 01:14:54.270
This could, of course, be
applied to other muscle groups.

01:14:54.270 --> 01:14:57.060
Let's say you want to
improve hamstring flexibility

01:14:57.060 --> 01:15:00.040
and limb range of motion
about and around the hamstring

01:15:01.040 --> 01:15:02.440
and involving the hamstring,

01:15:03.660 --> 01:15:05.920
you would want to do three sets

01:15:06.770 --> 01:15:09.060
of static stretching for the hamstring.

01:15:09.060 --> 01:15:11.953
Again, easy to find such
exercises on the internet.

01:15:13.730 --> 01:15:18.240
You would do that by holding
the stretch for 30 seconds,

01:15:18.240 --> 01:15:19.750
resting some period of time,

01:15:19.750 --> 01:15:21.830
and doing it again,
holding for 30 seconds,

01:15:21.830 --> 01:15:23.140
resting some period of time,

01:15:23.140 --> 01:15:26.310
and then holding it for 30 seconds.

01:15:26.310 --> 01:15:28.730
That would be one training
session for the hamstrings.

01:15:28.730 --> 01:15:30.830
I have to imagine that you'd
probably want to stretch

01:15:30.830 --> 01:15:33.550
other muscle groups as
well in that same session.

01:15:33.550 --> 01:15:34.990
Although, at least as far as I could tell,

01:15:34.990 --> 01:15:37.000
there was no data pointing to the fact

01:15:37.000 --> 01:15:38.900
that you couldn't do
your hamstring stretching

01:15:38.900 --> 01:15:39.733
one part of the day

01:15:39.733 --> 01:15:42.020
and your quadricep stretching
another part of the day.

01:15:42.020 --> 01:15:44.210
But presumably, you're
going to want to combine

01:15:44.210 --> 01:15:47.950
your flexibility training
into one single session.

01:15:47.950 --> 01:15:52.210
So, three sets of 30 seconds each

01:15:52.210 --> 01:15:53.610
get 90 seconds,

01:15:53.610 --> 01:15:57.000
and you would do that
ideally five times a week

01:15:57.000 --> 01:15:58.060
or maybe even more

01:15:58.060 --> 01:15:59.720
because it does seem like frequency

01:15:59.720 --> 01:16:02.030
distributed throughout the
week is an important parameter.

01:16:02.030 --> 01:16:04.470
Now, one thing that we
have not highlighted

01:16:04.470 --> 01:16:05.690
or at least described

01:16:05.690 --> 01:16:09.180
is how long to rest
between stretching sets.

01:16:09.180 --> 01:16:11.360
And despite my efforts,

01:16:11.360 --> 01:16:15.590
I could not find
research-backed information

01:16:15.590 --> 01:16:18.780
that pointed to whether
or not 30 seconds of rest

01:16:18.780 --> 01:16:20.180
for every 30 seconds stretching,

01:16:20.180 --> 01:16:24.830
or 60 seconds rest for every
30 second stretching was ideal.

01:16:24.830 --> 01:16:26.300
I think it's reasonable to assume

01:16:26.300 --> 01:16:28.960
that doubling the amount of time

01:16:28.960 --> 01:16:30.740
for the interleaving rest

01:16:30.740 --> 01:16:33.740
would be appropriate or at least doable.

01:16:33.740 --> 01:16:35.980
If anyone out there has
knowledge about rest

01:16:35.980 --> 01:16:39.460
between stretching sets
and has some physiology,

01:16:39.460 --> 01:16:42.750
or some biology, or some
experiential information

01:16:42.750 --> 01:16:47.010
as to why a given ratio of
duration of static stretch

01:16:47.010 --> 01:16:50.260
to rest in between static
stretch sets ought to be used,

01:16:50.260 --> 01:16:52.420
please put it in the comments on YouTube,

01:16:52.420 --> 01:16:54.490
that'd be a terrific way for
us to get that information.

01:16:54.490 --> 01:16:55.550
I'd love to do any follow-up

01:16:55.550 --> 01:16:58.660
to links that you provide and so on.

01:16:58.660 --> 01:17:02.090
But now, we're starting
to build into a protocol

01:17:02.090 --> 01:17:04.950
that is backed by the scientific data.

01:17:04.950 --> 01:17:07.630
Three sets of 30 seconds of holds

01:17:07.630 --> 01:17:11.410
done five times or maybe
even six times per week.

01:17:11.410 --> 01:17:14.770
One thing that did show
up in my exploration

01:17:14.770 --> 01:17:16.300
of the peer-reviewed research

01:17:16.300 --> 01:17:18.410
is this notion of warming up for all this.

01:17:18.410 --> 01:17:20.750
We haven't talked about that yet.

01:17:20.750 --> 01:17:22.160
In general, to avoid injury,

01:17:22.160 --> 01:17:24.470
it's a good idea to raise your
core body temperature a bit

01:17:24.470 --> 01:17:27.150
before doing these kinds of stretches.

01:17:27.150 --> 01:17:28.460
Even these static stretches,

01:17:28.460 --> 01:17:29.900
which we can sort of ease into

01:17:29.900 --> 01:17:33.870
and don't involve ballistic
movement by definition.

01:17:33.870 --> 01:17:37.470
And the basic take away
that I was able to find

01:17:37.470 --> 01:17:41.250
was that if we are
already warm from running,

01:17:41.250 --> 01:17:44.480
or from weight training,
or from some other activity

01:17:44.480 --> 01:17:46.670
that doing the static stretching

01:17:46.670 --> 01:17:49.510
practice at the end of
that weight training,

01:17:49.510 --> 01:17:51.900
or cardiovascular, or
other physical session

01:17:51.900 --> 01:17:55.600
would allow us to go immediately
into the stretching session

01:17:55.600 --> 01:17:57.840
because we're already warm, so to speak.

01:17:57.840 --> 01:18:01.570
Otherwise, raising one's core
body temperature by a bit

01:18:01.570 --> 01:18:04.660
by doing five to seven,
maybe even 10 minutes

01:18:04.660 --> 01:18:08.450
of easy cardiovascular exercise
or calisthenic movements,

01:18:08.450 --> 01:18:11.130
provided you can do those
without getting injured,

01:18:11.130 --> 01:18:13.720
seems to be an ideal way to
warm up the body for stretching.

01:18:13.720 --> 01:18:16.560
We should be warm or warm-up to stretch,

01:18:16.560 --> 01:18:17.393
although those warm-ups

01:18:17.393 --> 01:18:19.230
don't have to be extremely extensive.

01:18:19.230 --> 01:18:20.900
And then, just by way of logic,

01:18:20.900 --> 01:18:23.680
doing the static stretching
after resistance training

01:18:23.680 --> 01:18:26.340
or cardiovascular training
seems to be most beneficial.

01:18:26.340 --> 01:18:28.460
In fact, and unfortunately
we don't have time

01:18:28.460 --> 01:18:30.450
to go into this in too much detail today,

01:18:30.450 --> 01:18:34.260
I was able to find a number of
papers that make the argument

01:18:34.260 --> 01:18:38.100
that static stretching prior
to cardiovascular training

01:18:38.100 --> 01:18:41.840
and maybe even prior
to resistance training

01:18:41.840 --> 01:18:43.620
can limit our performance

01:18:43.620 --> 01:18:46.090
in running and resistance training.

01:18:46.090 --> 01:18:48.290
I realize that's a controversial area,

01:18:48.290 --> 01:18:51.350
you have those who say, "No,
it's immensely beneficial,"

01:18:51.350 --> 01:18:53.957
you have those who say, "No,
it inhibits performance,"

01:18:53.957 --> 01:18:56.220
and those that say, "No, it's a matter

01:18:56.220 --> 01:18:58.077
of how exactly you perform
that static stretching,

01:18:58.077 --> 01:19:00.230
and which muscle groups,
and how you're doing this,

01:19:00.230 --> 01:19:02.380
and how much time in between

01:19:02.380 --> 01:19:03.750
static stretching and performance."

01:19:03.750 --> 01:19:05.700
But to leave all that aside,

01:19:05.700 --> 01:19:09.570
doing static stretching after
some other form of exercise

01:19:09.570 --> 01:19:12.860
and if not after some form of exercise,

01:19:12.860 --> 01:19:15.550
after a brief warm-up to raise
your core body temperature

01:19:15.550 --> 01:19:17.550
definitely seems like the right way to go.

01:19:17.550 --> 01:19:18.680
Now, for some of you out there,

01:19:18.680 --> 01:19:20.720
and I confess for me as well,

01:19:20.720 --> 01:19:22.180
doing something five days a week

01:19:22.180 --> 01:19:23.230
seems like a big commitment,

01:19:23.230 --> 01:19:26.020
even if that commitment is
one to only do three sets

01:19:26.020 --> 01:19:28.860
of 30 second static stretches.

01:19:28.860 --> 01:19:31.360
I say this because you've got the warm-up,

01:19:31.360 --> 01:19:33.500
I generally like to
bring a kind of a focus

01:19:33.500 --> 01:19:35.100
and dedication to a practice.

01:19:35.100 --> 01:19:39.143
And, of course, because when
doing these kinds of protocols,

01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:41.940
it's likely that you're not
just stretching your hamstring,

01:19:41.940 --> 01:19:43.700
so it's not just 90 seconds of work

01:19:43.700 --> 01:19:45.580
with a minute of rest in between,

01:19:45.580 --> 01:19:48.480
but very likely that we're also
doing quadricep stretching,

01:19:48.480 --> 01:19:50.250
and also doing stretching
for the shoulders,

01:19:50.250 --> 01:19:52.360
and stretching for the back,
and the neck, and so on.

01:19:52.360 --> 01:19:54.830
And so, that entire session
is going to take some time,

01:19:54.830 --> 01:19:57.100
and five days a week is a
pretty serious commitment

01:19:57.100 --> 01:20:00.250
for most, especially for those
of us that don't exercise

01:20:00.250 --> 01:20:03.660
or do athletics for a
living, which I don't.

01:20:03.660 --> 01:20:06.400
So, there is some evidence
from the literature

01:20:06.400 --> 01:20:08.060
that one can get away with,

01:20:08.060 --> 01:20:09.690
or I don't even know that
we should think about it

01:20:09.690 --> 01:20:10.523
as getting away with,

01:20:10.523 --> 01:20:14.150
but that one can do longer
hold static stretches

01:20:14.150 --> 01:20:17.290
of up to say 60 seconds,

01:20:17.290 --> 01:20:20.970
but do fewer total sessions per week.

01:20:20.970 --> 01:20:24.260
So, rather than three
30 second static holds,

01:20:24.260 --> 01:20:27.530
doing three 60 second static holds

01:20:27.530 --> 01:20:30.140
and doing those every other day.

01:20:30.140 --> 01:20:31.580
And there really hasn't been

01:20:31.580 --> 01:20:33.130
a systematic exploration of this.

01:20:33.130 --> 01:20:36.233
The article that I was referring
to just a few moments ago,

01:20:37.330 --> 01:20:40.730
this analysis of the 23 articles

01:20:40.730 --> 01:20:43.150
was combined into this
enormous set of tables

01:20:43.150 --> 01:20:45.350
and some really quite nice graphs

01:20:45.350 --> 01:20:46.770
that you're welcome to look at

01:20:46.770 --> 01:20:49.090
since we're going to
provide a link to the study.

01:20:49.090 --> 01:20:52.080
There are a couple of key take
aways that I want to mention

01:20:52.080 --> 01:20:53.270
that are separate from this issue

01:20:53.270 --> 01:20:55.970
of how long to stretch and how often.

01:20:55.970 --> 01:20:59.640
First of all, they describe
in their discussion

01:20:59.640 --> 01:21:02.450
that there were improvements
in range of motion

01:21:03.340 --> 01:21:07.080
independent of whether or not
people did static stretching,

01:21:07.080 --> 01:21:08.830
active stretching, passive stretching,

01:21:08.830 --> 01:21:11.970
ballistic stretching, or PNF stretching.

01:21:11.970 --> 01:21:13.650
So, all of those forms of stretching

01:21:13.650 --> 01:21:15.510
will improve limb range of motion.

01:21:15.510 --> 01:21:17.170
This is essential to point out

01:21:17.170 --> 01:21:19.330
and I want to emphasize this.

01:21:19.330 --> 01:21:20.863
Static stretching, however,

01:21:21.770 --> 01:21:25.950
gave the greatest degree of
gains in limb range of motion.

01:21:25.950 --> 01:21:29.790
And on average, they saw a 20.9% increase,

01:21:29.790 --> 01:21:32.240
but some of the other
increases they observed

01:21:32.240 --> 01:21:34.190
were also quite substantial.

01:21:34.190 --> 01:21:36.680
So, ballistic stretching can also provide

01:21:36.680 --> 01:21:40.530
some pretty impressive limb
range of motion improvements.

01:21:40.530 --> 01:21:42.160
However, they tended to be in the range

01:21:42.160 --> 01:21:45.960
of, here they point out, 11.65% increase,

01:21:45.960 --> 01:21:48.400
or in the case of PNF, a 15% increase.

01:21:48.400 --> 01:21:51.300
So, it appears that the
greatest improvements

01:21:51.300 --> 01:21:55.200
in limb range of motion for
your time spent and effort spent

01:21:55.200 --> 01:21:58.600
is going to be this minimum
of five minutes per week

01:21:58.600 --> 01:22:00.140
to elicit a significant response

01:22:00.140 --> 01:22:01.680
with five days being the minimum

01:22:01.680 --> 01:22:03.260
weekly recommended frequency

01:22:03.260 --> 01:22:07.460
to achieve significant range
of motion improvements.

01:22:07.460 --> 01:22:10.350
I confess, this was
pretty surprising to me

01:22:10.350 --> 01:22:13.260
when I compare flexibility training

01:22:13.260 --> 01:22:16.410
to, say, resistance training
for strength and hypertrophy.

01:22:16.410 --> 01:22:17.400
I've had the experience,

01:22:17.400 --> 01:22:19.130
and I know that other people
have had the experience,

01:22:19.130 --> 01:22:23.290
and I think Dr. Andy
Galpin would probably agree

01:22:23.290 --> 01:22:27.110
that provided one trains
hard enough and appropriately

01:22:27.110 --> 01:22:30.730
that you don't need to
train resistance training

01:22:30.730 --> 01:22:33.780
five days a week in order to
get significant improvements

01:22:33.780 --> 01:22:34.880
in strength and hypertrophy.

01:22:34.880 --> 01:22:36.220
Some people might need to,

01:22:36.220 --> 01:22:39.100
but you can get a lot of positive results

01:22:39.100 --> 01:22:42.190
in those variables with
less frequent training,

01:22:42.190 --> 01:22:45.640
certainly with three or four
days a week of training.

01:22:45.640 --> 01:22:47.290
And for cardiovascular training,

01:22:47.290 --> 01:22:49.240
I'm not aware of anyone having tested

01:22:49.240 --> 01:22:51.410
whether or not one very long run each week

01:22:51.410 --> 01:22:53.270
can actually increase
cardiovascular fitness

01:22:53.270 --> 01:22:54.350
and you're not doing anything else.

01:22:54.350 --> 01:22:55.970
Although, I have to
imagine you'd probably see

01:22:55.970 --> 01:22:58.060
some improvement compared
to not doing anything,

01:22:58.060 --> 01:23:01.670
but most people are doing
repeated training sessions

01:23:01.670 --> 01:23:04.130
of cardiovascular strength training.

01:23:04.130 --> 01:23:06.900
Not a lot of people are
doing five days a week

01:23:06.900 --> 01:23:09.530
of strength training, at
least that I'm aware of.

01:23:09.530 --> 01:23:12.170
Some people are, but most
people I think are not.

01:23:12.170 --> 01:23:15.140
And some people are
doing five or more days

01:23:15.140 --> 01:23:17.440
a week of cardiovascular training.

01:23:17.440 --> 01:23:20.710
I'm guessing that most people
are not doing five days a week

01:23:20.710 --> 01:23:22.680
of dedicated static stretch

01:23:22.680 --> 01:23:25.770
range of motion directed training,

01:23:25.770 --> 01:23:29.250
but it does appear that that
frequency about the week

01:23:29.250 --> 01:23:30.490
getting those repeated sessions,

01:23:30.490 --> 01:23:32.820
even if they are short for
an individual muscle group,

01:23:32.820 --> 01:23:34.040
turns out to be important.

01:23:34.040 --> 01:23:37.490
And so, that points to perhaps the reason

01:23:37.490 --> 01:23:40.510
why so few people are doing
dedicated range of motion work,

01:23:40.510 --> 01:23:44.590
but it also reminds me
that all of the studies

01:23:44.590 --> 01:23:46.640
that were described at
least in this review

01:23:46.640 --> 01:23:48.730
and some of the other ones that were not

01:23:48.730 --> 01:23:52.160
really show impressive changes
in limb range of motion.

01:23:52.160 --> 01:23:56.180
I mean, 20+%, or even 15% with PNF,

01:23:56.180 --> 01:23:59.070
I mean, these are big changes
that are going to benefit us,

01:23:59.070 --> 01:24:01.500
they're going to offset
the age-related losses

01:24:01.500 --> 01:24:03.320
in flexibility, for sure,

01:24:03.320 --> 01:24:05.580
if one is dedicated about these practices.

01:24:05.580 --> 01:24:07.300
And in many cases,

01:24:07.300 --> 01:24:09.500
they're going to increase
limb range of motion

01:24:09.500 --> 01:24:12.300
in ways that are going to
allow us better performance

01:24:12.300 --> 01:24:15.183
in certain physical endeavors,
certainly better balance.

01:24:15.183 --> 01:24:16.060
All right, we haven't really talked

01:24:16.060 --> 01:24:17.330
about balance and stability,

01:24:17.330 --> 01:24:21.230
but range of motion can
impair balance and stability

01:24:21.230 --> 01:24:22.770
in some extreme circumstances,

01:24:22.770 --> 01:24:25.590
but by and large, limb range
of motion, lack of tightness,

01:24:25.590 --> 01:24:29.300
improved posture, improved
physical performance, excuse me.

01:24:29.300 --> 01:24:31.410
And things of that sort is something

01:24:31.410 --> 01:24:32.717
that I think we can all benefit from,

01:24:32.717 --> 01:24:35.630
and that are key features of longevity.

01:24:35.630 --> 01:24:36.660
We don't often think of them

01:24:36.660 --> 01:24:39.040
because we so prioritize
cardiovascular health

01:24:39.040 --> 01:24:40.960
and the relationship between
the heart and brain health,

01:24:40.960 --> 01:24:45.870
and resistance training, and
musculoskeletal hypertrophy,

01:24:45.870 --> 01:24:47.160
or strength, et cetera.

01:24:47.160 --> 01:24:49.470
But as I delved into this literature,

01:24:49.470 --> 01:24:51.250
it really highlighted for me the extent

01:24:51.250 --> 01:24:54.820
to which having really
good limb range of motion,

01:24:54.820 --> 01:24:56.920
at least maintaining limb range of motion

01:24:56.920 --> 01:24:59.020
as we age from year to year,

01:24:59.020 --> 01:25:01.440
and maybe even improving
limb range of motion

01:25:01.440 --> 01:25:04.730
can be immensely beneficial
for reducing pain

01:25:04.730 --> 01:25:07.190
for, again, improving posture,

01:25:07.190 --> 01:25:10.240
improving our ability to
perform, to walk, et cetera.

01:25:10.240 --> 01:25:12.450
And indeed, there's a whole literature

01:25:12.450 --> 01:25:14.680
that relates our limb range of motion

01:25:14.680 --> 01:25:16.430
to things like pain management

01:25:16.430 --> 01:25:19.180
of things related to headache
and so on and so forth.

01:25:19.180 --> 01:25:21.820
So, limb range of motion is not just about

01:25:21.820 --> 01:25:23.010
becoming a contortionist

01:25:23.010 --> 01:25:25.330
or being able to complete the yoga class,

01:25:25.330 --> 01:25:27.860
it really is about maintaining
the integrity and the health

01:25:27.860 --> 01:25:31.390
of the neuromuscular system,
the connective tissue,

01:25:31.390 --> 01:25:33.880
and the neuromuscular connective network

01:25:33.880 --> 01:25:36.310
because those are indeed
working as an ecosystem

01:25:36.310 --> 01:25:37.190
and a network.

01:25:37.190 --> 01:25:38.490
I'd like to just briefly touch

01:25:38.490 --> 01:25:40.773
on PNF stretching for a moment.

01:25:42.330 --> 01:25:43.830
Again, this is a vast landscape

01:25:43.830 --> 01:25:45.830
with many parameters and
different practitioners,

01:25:45.830 --> 01:25:49.763
a lot of competing opinions out there,

01:25:50.950 --> 01:25:52.290
to put it lightly.

01:25:52.290 --> 01:25:55.720
Nonetheless, I do want to
emphasize that the PNF training

01:25:55.720 --> 01:25:57.530
leverages those spindle mechanisms

01:25:57.530 --> 01:26:00.590
and GTO mechanisms that
we talked about earlier,

01:26:00.590 --> 01:26:03.920
but I realize that in describing
the quadricep contraction,

01:26:03.920 --> 01:26:06.830
hamstring stretch little mini-experiment

01:26:06.830 --> 01:26:08.750
that hopefully you did,

01:26:08.750 --> 01:26:11.240
that I didn't really highlight
the role of the GTOs,

01:26:11.240 --> 01:26:13.270
the Golgi tendon organs that much.

01:26:13.270 --> 01:26:16.270
And I just would like to just
briefly do that for a moment.

01:26:16.270 --> 01:26:20.340
The GTOs have multiple functions.

01:26:20.340 --> 01:26:22.110
In fact, I think even though GTOs

01:26:22.110 --> 01:26:24.770
are in every medical textbook,
every physiology textbook,

01:26:24.770 --> 01:26:27.690
every first year neuroscientist
learns about them

01:26:27.690 --> 01:26:29.460
when learning about the
neuromuscular junctions

01:26:29.460 --> 01:26:32.210
and the mechanisms of
interoception, et cetera,

01:26:32.210 --> 01:26:34.670
they're likely to have
other functions as well.

01:26:34.670 --> 01:26:38.253
And one of the reasons why
PNF stretching does work,

01:26:39.750 --> 01:26:42.330
whether or not you're
doing that by using a strap

01:26:42.330 --> 01:26:44.040
to pull back a limb,

01:26:44.040 --> 01:26:46.450
or whether or not you're
actively contracting

01:26:46.450 --> 01:26:50.270
your quadriceps to then
release and emphasize

01:26:50.270 --> 01:26:52.620
stretch range of motion
for your hamstrings

01:26:52.620 --> 01:26:57.620
and related muscle groups is
that activation of those GTOs,

01:26:58.060 --> 01:27:02.540
meaning putting loads and
tension into that system

01:27:02.540 --> 01:27:04.830
can inhibit the spindles

01:27:04.830 --> 01:27:07.530
in the opposite
antagonistic muscle groups.

01:27:07.530 --> 01:27:11.320
Okay, so one of the reasons why flexing,

01:27:11.320 --> 01:27:14.500
or I should say
contracting your quadriceps

01:27:14.500 --> 01:27:16.750
really intensely for some period of time

01:27:16.750 --> 01:27:20.000
allows your hamstrings to
subsequently experience

01:27:20.000 --> 01:27:21.660
greater range of motion.

01:27:21.660 --> 01:27:23.040
And again, it's not just the hamstrings,

01:27:23.040 --> 01:27:24.810
but the related connective tissue

01:27:24.810 --> 01:27:27.160
and neural circuits, et cetera

01:27:27.160 --> 01:27:32.110
is because yes, it's
quote-unquote relaxing

01:27:32.110 --> 01:27:34.270
the hamstrings and the spindle,

01:27:34.270 --> 01:27:37.960
but there's also a direct relationship

01:27:37.960 --> 01:27:42.100
between activation of
the GTOs in the quadricep

01:27:42.100 --> 01:27:45.800
and release of the spindles

01:27:45.800 --> 01:27:47.420
in the hamstring and related muscles.

01:27:47.420 --> 01:27:49.950
This has a name, it's
called autogenic inhibition,

01:27:49.950 --> 01:27:53.730
it's a fancy name for
contraction of one muscle group

01:27:53.730 --> 01:27:56.320
providing a relaxation
of the other muscle group

01:27:57.160 --> 01:27:59.310
that's antagonistic to it.

01:27:59.310 --> 01:28:00.930
And it relates back to this idea

01:28:00.930 --> 01:28:02.660
of interleaving sets in the gym.

01:28:02.660 --> 01:28:05.080
So, if you think back to that example,

01:28:05.080 --> 01:28:08.320
now it should make sense
as to why, for instance,

01:28:08.320 --> 01:28:10.780
if you do, let's say,
a set of bench presses

01:28:10.780 --> 01:28:12.100
or shoulder presses,

01:28:12.100 --> 01:28:14.500
and you let's say you get 10 repetitions

01:28:14.500 --> 01:28:16.060
and you fail on the 11th,

01:28:16.060 --> 01:28:18.340
that muscle is very, very fatigued.

01:28:18.340 --> 01:28:20.270
If you were to rest some period of time

01:28:20.270 --> 01:28:22.070
and then go back and do another set,

01:28:22.910 --> 01:28:26.120
well, during the rest, that
muscle group has been relaxing,

01:28:26.120 --> 01:28:27.670
it's obviously not
contracting the same way

01:28:27.670 --> 01:28:29.870
it was during the resistance set,

01:28:29.870 --> 01:28:32.580
but by going and doing a pulling exercise

01:28:32.580 --> 01:28:34.600
that involves the
antagonistic muscle group,

01:28:34.600 --> 01:28:37.360
so strongly contracting the
back muscles through a pull

01:28:37.360 --> 01:28:41.660
like a pull-down, or a chin-up,
or a row-type exercise,

01:28:41.660 --> 01:28:46.030
you're activating or near
activating the GTO system

01:28:46.030 --> 01:28:47.890
in those pulling muscles in a way

01:28:47.890 --> 01:28:52.810
that provides autogenic inhibition
for the pushing muscles.

01:28:52.810 --> 01:28:55.530
Now, again, the physios out there

01:28:55.530 --> 01:28:57.410
are probably either screaming

01:28:57.410 --> 01:28:59.980
or banging their heads
against whatever sound system

01:28:59.980 --> 01:29:02.690
this happens to be arriving
through to them saying, "Wait,

01:29:02.690 --> 01:29:05.840
but in many cases, the GTOs
aren't activated enough

01:29:05.840 --> 01:29:07.630
to provide that autogenic inhibition."

01:29:07.630 --> 01:29:11.710
That's true, but even the
sub-threshold activation

01:29:11.710 --> 01:29:13.520
of those intraspinal circuits,

01:29:13.520 --> 01:29:15.750
so the place where the GTO circuit

01:29:15.750 --> 01:29:17.540
and the spindle circuit interact,

01:29:17.540 --> 01:29:20.920
can provide an additional replenishment

01:29:20.920 --> 01:29:22.940
of, say, the pushing muscles

01:29:22.940 --> 01:29:24.760
while you're activating
those pulling muscles.

01:29:24.760 --> 01:29:26.710
And this is at least one, not the only,

01:29:26.710 --> 01:29:28.100
but at least one mechanism

01:29:28.100 --> 01:29:30.970
by which interleaving push
and pull, push and pull

01:29:30.970 --> 01:29:33.110
for both strength and
hypertrophy training,

01:29:33.110 --> 01:29:37.560
but also for range of motion
stretching-type training

01:29:37.560 --> 01:29:40.670
can allow you to achieve better results

01:29:40.670 --> 01:29:42.340
in a shorter period of time.

01:29:42.340 --> 01:29:44.990
And I raise this because
I want to keep in mind

01:29:44.990 --> 01:29:47.270
the efficiency of any training program.

01:29:47.270 --> 01:29:50.490
We just a moment ago
established that doing,

01:29:50.490 --> 01:29:53.320
for example, three sets
of 30 second static holds

01:29:53.320 --> 01:29:54.940
can be very useful for the hamstrings

01:29:54.940 --> 01:29:59.020
with let's just say for sake
of simplicity and practicality

01:29:59.020 --> 01:30:00.670
a minute's rest in between.

01:30:00.670 --> 01:30:02.530
But during that minute's rest,

01:30:02.530 --> 01:30:06.390
you can stretch the opposite
antagonistic muscle group,

01:30:06.390 --> 01:30:08.380
such as the quadriceps,

01:30:08.380 --> 01:30:10.770
or if you want to use PNF training,

01:30:10.770 --> 01:30:13.840
you could do loading of
the quadriceps in between.

01:30:13.840 --> 01:30:15.060
So, there are a number of different ways

01:30:15.060 --> 01:30:19.130
in which you can start to
interleave static stretching

01:30:19.130 --> 01:30:20.870
with PNF stretching,

01:30:20.870 --> 01:30:24.550
you can start to interleave
even PNF-type protocols

01:30:24.550 --> 01:30:25.550
with resistance training,

01:30:25.550 --> 01:30:27.800
although that gets a bit more complicated.

01:30:27.800 --> 01:30:29.260
You can really start to construct

01:30:29.260 --> 01:30:31.833
and build protocols
that are ideal for you.

01:30:32.740 --> 01:30:33.573
What we will do

01:30:33.573 --> 01:30:36.890
is for an upcoming Neural
Network Newsletter.

01:30:36.890 --> 01:30:38.200
So, for those of you that aren't familiar,

01:30:38.200 --> 01:30:39.220
the Huberman Lab Podcast

01:30:39.220 --> 01:30:40.960
has a so-called Neural Network Newsletter,

01:30:40.960 --> 01:30:44.570
these are monthly newsletters
where we put distilled points

01:30:44.570 --> 01:30:47.010
from the podcast and oftentimes protocols

01:30:47.010 --> 01:30:48.870
in a downloadable PDF form.

01:30:48.870 --> 01:30:50.410
You can access it by giving us your email,

01:30:50.410 --> 01:30:51.910
we don't share your email with anybody.

01:30:51.910 --> 01:30:53.120
If you want to see examples of these,

01:30:53.120 --> 01:30:54.610
you can go to hubermanlab.com

01:30:54.610 --> 01:30:56.310
and go to the menu and see Newsletter,

01:30:56.310 --> 01:30:57.400
you don't have to sign up for anything

01:30:57.400 --> 01:30:59.520
to see examples of what these are like.

01:30:59.520 --> 01:31:01.920
I'll provide a couple
of different protocols,

01:31:01.920 --> 01:31:04.670
one that is pure static stretching,

01:31:04.670 --> 01:31:07.840
one that involves PNF-type stretching,

01:31:07.840 --> 01:31:10.830
and I'll also put down a protocol

01:31:10.830 --> 01:31:14.030
that involves the antagonistic
interleaved muscle training

01:31:14.030 --> 01:31:15.200
of the sort that I've been describing

01:31:15.200 --> 01:31:16.940
a few times throughout this episode.

01:31:16.940 --> 01:31:18.810
And then, you can try and apply those

01:31:18.810 --> 01:31:21.700
either separately or maybe
combine them in some way

01:31:21.700 --> 01:31:23.250
that's useful for your goals.

01:31:23.250 --> 01:31:25.480
There are a couple of key
elements that are essential

01:31:25.480 --> 01:31:28.970
for building a safe and
effective range of motion

01:31:28.970 --> 01:31:31.460
increasing program that arrived to us

01:31:31.460 --> 01:31:33.280
both through the peer-reviewed research

01:31:33.280 --> 01:31:36.020
and, admittedly, from people
that have been involved

01:31:36.020 --> 01:31:38.540
in teaching and training range of motion

01:31:38.540 --> 01:31:40.380
for a very long period of time.

01:31:40.380 --> 01:31:41.630
Some of you may be familiar

01:31:41.630 --> 01:31:43.220
with the so-called Anderson method,

01:31:43.220 --> 01:31:45.210
it's been around for a long time.

01:31:45.210 --> 01:31:46.730
Actually have never met Anderson,

01:31:46.730 --> 01:31:48.040
I don't, I should know this,

01:31:48.040 --> 01:31:49.320
I don't even know if he's still alive,

01:31:49.320 --> 01:31:50.510
I hope he's still alive,

01:31:50.510 --> 01:31:53.100
but in any event, there are
a lot of different features

01:31:53.100 --> 01:31:55.210
to the Anderson and other protocols.

01:31:55.210 --> 01:31:57.760
But one of the aspects
of the Anderson protocol

01:31:57.760 --> 01:31:59.360
that I think is highly relevant.

01:31:59.360 --> 01:32:01.540
In fact, I know is relevant
to the peer-reviewed research

01:32:01.540 --> 01:32:03.620
that we're going to talk
about in a few moments

01:32:03.620 --> 01:32:06.870
is this notion of pushing through pain,

01:32:06.870 --> 01:32:10.790
and how active or how passive
to be about static stretching.

01:32:10.790 --> 01:32:12.610
Now, this is somewhat subjective.

01:32:12.610 --> 01:32:15.730
Right, if you think about
getting into a stretch,

01:32:15.730 --> 01:32:17.590
again, we'll just use the
hamstrings for example.

01:32:17.590 --> 01:32:21.520
So, you're either reaching
for your toes while seated,

01:32:21.520 --> 01:32:23.870
or maybe you're using a strap

01:32:23.870 --> 01:32:27.140
and you're raising your foot
overhead while lying down,

01:32:27.140 --> 01:32:31.253
or maybe you're doing a
toe touch-type exercise.

01:32:32.830 --> 01:32:34.070
How far should you reach?

01:32:34.070 --> 01:32:36.270
Where is the end range of motion?

01:32:36.270 --> 01:32:37.810
Should you balance?
Should you not balance?

01:32:37.810 --> 01:32:40.060
We're going to talk a little
bit more about that in a moment,

01:32:40.060 --> 01:32:42.700
but Anderson has an
interesting idea and principle,

01:32:42.700 --> 01:32:45.010
which has thread through
a lot of his teachings

01:32:45.010 --> 01:32:47.890
that I think are very much
in keeping with the study

01:32:47.890 --> 01:32:49.710
that I'm about to describe next,

01:32:49.710 --> 01:32:53.220
where he emphasizes to yes,

01:32:53.220 --> 01:32:56.560
to stretch to the end
of the range of motion,

01:32:56.560 --> 01:32:59.370
but not to focus so much on
where that range of motion

01:32:59.370 --> 01:33:01.350
happens to be that day.

01:33:01.350 --> 01:33:04.060
So, for instance, not thinking, "Oh,

01:33:04.060 --> 01:33:06.920
I can always touch my toes, for instance.

01:33:06.920 --> 01:33:09.290
And therefore that's the starting place

01:33:09.290 --> 01:33:11.860
for my flexibility training today."

01:33:11.860 --> 01:33:15.760
But rather to take the entirety
of your system into account

01:33:15.760 --> 01:33:17.370
each day and understand that, okay,

01:33:17.370 --> 01:33:19.810
provided your warmed up appropriately,

01:33:19.810 --> 01:33:21.460
that you're now going to
stretch your hamstrings,

01:33:21.460 --> 01:33:24.540
for instance, and you're going
to reach down for your toes,

01:33:24.540 --> 01:33:27.610
but that your range of motion
might be adjusted that day

01:33:27.610 --> 01:33:29.720
by way of tension and stress,

01:33:29.720 --> 01:33:31.960
or by way of ambient
temperature in the room.

01:33:31.960 --> 01:33:34.690
And to basically define
the end range of motion

01:33:34.690 --> 01:33:37.820
as the place where you
can feel the stretch

01:33:37.820 --> 01:33:39.420
in the relevant muscle groups.

01:33:39.420 --> 01:33:40.560
I think this is important

01:33:40.560 --> 01:33:43.200
because unlike resistance training

01:33:43.200 --> 01:33:44.820
or cardiovascular training,

01:33:44.820 --> 01:33:47.440
where we can measure
distance traveled over time

01:33:47.440 --> 01:33:48.900
in the case of cardiovascular training,

01:33:48.900 --> 01:33:50.550
or how much weight is on the bar,

01:33:50.550 --> 01:33:52.900
and count repetitions, et cetera.

01:33:52.900 --> 01:33:54.890
With range of motion training,

01:33:54.890 --> 01:33:57.020
of course, range of motion is the feature

01:33:57.020 --> 01:33:58.230
that we're interested in,

01:33:58.230 --> 01:34:02.250
but there is likely to be a lot
of variation from day-to-day

01:34:02.250 --> 01:34:04.090
based on a number of different internal

01:34:04.090 --> 01:34:05.540
and external factors.

01:34:05.540 --> 01:34:06.900
And so, the Anderson method

01:34:06.900 --> 01:34:08.890
is really about getting into static

01:34:08.890 --> 01:34:10.530
and other forms of stretching.

01:34:10.530 --> 01:34:12.120
I think today we've mainly been focusing

01:34:12.120 --> 01:34:15.350
on static stretching and
holding the end range of motion,

01:34:15.350 --> 01:34:17.947
but really paying attention
to the feel of the stretch

01:34:17.947 --> 01:34:19.510
and the muscles involved.

01:34:19.510 --> 01:34:21.560
And there are parallels in resistance

01:34:21.560 --> 01:34:23.850
and cardiovascular training
too I realize, right?

01:34:23.850 --> 01:34:26.260
In the case of trying
to build hypertrophy,

01:34:26.260 --> 01:34:29.200
or I should say improve
hypertrophy, muscle size,

01:34:29.200 --> 01:34:32.220
oftentimes the best
advice that one can give

01:34:32.220 --> 01:34:33.970
is to don't try to lift weights,

01:34:33.970 --> 01:34:35.440
but rather to challenge muscles.

01:34:35.440 --> 01:34:37.260
Now, of course, you need
to provide adequate loads

01:34:37.260 --> 01:34:38.800
in order to get hypertrophy,

01:34:38.800 --> 01:34:40.450
but when you're training
purely for strength,

01:34:40.450 --> 01:34:42.520
it's about moving weights.

01:34:42.520 --> 01:34:44.210
When you're training
purely for hypertrophy

01:34:44.210 --> 01:34:45.310
or mainly for hypertrophy,

01:34:45.310 --> 01:34:48.030
it's really about challenging
muscles using weights

01:34:48.030 --> 01:34:49.530
or other forms of resistance.

01:34:49.530 --> 01:34:54.080
And similarly, and in keeping
with this Anderson method,

01:34:54.080 --> 01:34:57.300
when trying to build limb range of motion,

01:34:57.300 --> 01:35:00.800
doing static stretching at a
place where it's difficult,

01:35:00.800 --> 01:35:03.810
but that you can experience
the stretch of the muscle

01:35:03.810 --> 01:35:07.820
cognitively, consciously being
able to focus on the muscles

01:35:07.820 --> 01:35:10.880
and their stretch is at least as useful

01:35:10.880 --> 01:35:13.880
as is evaluating the
current range of motion

01:35:13.880 --> 01:35:14.713
you're able to achieve.

01:35:14.713 --> 01:35:15.546
So, what does this mean?

01:35:15.546 --> 01:35:17.440
This means feel the muscles
as you stretch them,

01:35:17.440 --> 01:35:18.750
don't just go through the motions.

01:35:18.750 --> 01:35:21.390
And this means don't get so attached

01:35:21.390 --> 01:35:23.070
to being able to always achieve,

01:35:23.070 --> 01:35:25.730
for instance, a stretch
of a given distance

01:35:25.730 --> 01:35:26.930
within a given session.

01:35:26.930 --> 01:35:30.260
You might actually find that
by just finding the place

01:35:30.260 --> 01:35:32.150
where you can't get much further

01:35:32.150 --> 01:35:34.280
and holding the static stretch there,

01:35:34.280 --> 01:35:35.720
that on the second and third set

01:35:35.720 --> 01:35:36.860
that you happen to be doing that day,

01:35:36.860 --> 01:35:40.140
that your range of motion will
be increased considerably.

01:35:40.140 --> 01:35:42.610
Maybe not, but very likely yes, you will.

01:35:42.610 --> 01:35:46.220
And, of course, evaluating
range of motion over time

01:35:46.220 --> 01:35:47.360
is the key parameter

01:35:47.360 --> 01:35:49.680
because that's the goal
of all this type of work.

01:35:49.680 --> 01:35:52.950
Now, along these lines,

01:35:52.950 --> 01:35:55.270
there is this variable that
we've mentioned a few times

01:35:55.270 --> 01:35:57.380
of passive versus active stretching,

01:35:57.380 --> 01:36:00.380
and there's this even
more nebulous variable,

01:36:00.380 --> 01:36:02.150
this even more kind of subjective thing

01:36:02.150 --> 01:36:05.560
of how much effort to put into it?

01:36:05.560 --> 01:36:07.200
Should you push into the stretch?

01:36:07.200 --> 01:36:09.540
Would you even want to balance a tiny bit?

01:36:09.540 --> 01:36:12.630
Would you want to reach
into that end point

01:36:12.630 --> 01:36:16.000
and try and extend it within
a given set and session?

01:36:16.000 --> 01:36:18.180
And for that reason,

01:36:18.180 --> 01:36:20.550
I was excited to find this paper

01:36:20.550 --> 01:36:22.850
entitled A Comparison of
Two Stretching Modalities

01:36:22.850 --> 01:36:24.940
on Lower-Limb Range of Motion Measurements

01:36:24.940 --> 01:36:26.960
in Recreational Dancers.

01:36:26.960 --> 01:36:28.680
Happens to be done in
recreational dancers,

01:36:28.680 --> 01:36:30.720
it's a six-week intervention program

01:36:30.720 --> 01:36:32.690
that compared low-intensity stretching,

01:36:32.690 --> 01:36:34.950
which they call Microstretching.

01:36:34.950 --> 01:36:35.980
They used a capital M,

01:36:35.980 --> 01:36:37.980
so I don't know if that
means that it's proprietary,

01:36:37.980 --> 01:36:41.690
although I didn't see evidence
of a conflict of interest,

01:36:41.690 --> 01:36:43.460
but they call it Microstretching.

01:36:43.460 --> 01:36:46.537
But to be very clear,

01:36:46.537 --> 01:36:48.770
Microstretching in the
case of this manuscript

01:36:48.770 --> 01:36:50.850
is low-intensity stretching.

01:36:50.850 --> 01:36:51.683
And they compared that

01:36:51.683 --> 01:36:54.510
with moderate intensity static stretching

01:36:54.510 --> 01:36:56.920
on an active and passive ranges of motion.

01:36:56.920 --> 01:36:58.940
Okay, so there are a lot of
different variables are here,

01:36:58.940 --> 01:37:01.440
but I'll just highlight
a few of the things

01:37:01.440 --> 01:37:03.170
that are really most relevant to us,

01:37:03.170 --> 01:37:05.080
and I'll give you the
take away at the outset

01:37:05.080 --> 01:37:06.530
and then return to it at the end

01:37:06.530 --> 01:37:09.450
so that if I lose any of your attention

01:37:09.450 --> 01:37:10.410
in the next couple of minutes,

01:37:10.410 --> 01:37:14.140
at least you have that key take away.

01:37:14.140 --> 01:37:15.220
Basically, what they found

01:37:15.220 --> 01:37:17.050
was that a six-week training program

01:37:17.050 --> 01:37:19.820
using very low-intensity stretching

01:37:19.820 --> 01:37:22.930
had a greater positive effect
on lower limb range of motion

01:37:22.930 --> 01:37:26.090
than did moderate-intensity
static stretching.

01:37:26.090 --> 01:37:28.830
I find that incredibly
interesting, so very low intensity,

01:37:28.830 --> 01:37:31.080
and we'll define what
that means in a moment.

01:37:32.010 --> 01:37:33.587
Here, I'm quoting them,

01:37:33.587 --> 01:37:35.300
"The most interesting aspect of the study

01:37:35.300 --> 01:37:37.810
was the greater increase
in active range of motion

01:37:37.810 --> 01:37:39.390
compared to passive range of motion

01:37:39.390 --> 01:37:40.760
by the Microstretching group."

01:37:40.760 --> 01:37:42.670
So, this relates to what
we were just talking about

01:37:42.670 --> 01:37:45.830
a few moments ago as it
relates to the Anderson method,

01:37:45.830 --> 01:37:49.990
which is that very
low-intensity stretching,

01:37:49.990 --> 01:37:53.700
meaning effort that feels not painful

01:37:53.700 --> 01:37:58.700
and in fact might even feel
easy or at least not straining

01:37:59.480 --> 01:38:02.740
to exceed a given range of motion

01:38:02.740 --> 01:38:04.960
turns out to not just be as effective,

01:38:04.960 --> 01:38:08.680
but more effective than
moderate-intensity stretching.

01:38:08.680 --> 01:38:11.120
So, what is low-intensity
static stretching?

01:38:11.120 --> 01:38:13.670
Well, they define this as
the stretches were completed

01:38:13.670 --> 01:38:17.350
at an intensity of 30-40%

01:38:17.350 --> 01:38:20.870
where 100% equals the
point of pain, right?

01:38:20.870 --> 01:38:24.490
So, 30-40% in these individuals,

01:38:24.490 --> 01:38:25.680
and again, I'm paraphrasing,

01:38:25.680 --> 01:38:28.640
induced a relaxed state
within the individual

01:38:28.640 --> 01:38:29.730
and the specific muscle.

01:38:29.730 --> 01:38:31.710
And here they were holding
these static stretches

01:38:31.710 --> 01:38:35.370
I should mention for one
minute, not 30 seconds.

01:38:35.370 --> 01:38:36.900
Now, the control group

01:38:36.900 --> 01:38:39.590
was doing the exact same overall protocol,

01:38:39.590 --> 01:38:42.700
so daily stretching for six weeks,

01:38:42.700 --> 01:38:47.170
the same exercises, holding
each set for 60 seconds,

01:38:47.170 --> 01:38:51.350
but were using an
intensity of stretch of 80%

01:38:51.350 --> 01:38:54.720
where, again, 100
represents the point of pain

01:38:54.720 --> 01:38:58.280
or the point where the person
would want to stop stretching.

01:38:58.280 --> 01:38:59.940
I find these data incredibly interesting

01:38:59.940 --> 01:39:02.520
for, I think, what ought
to be obvious reasons.

01:39:02.520 --> 01:39:05.390
If you're going to embark on a flexibility

01:39:05.390 --> 01:39:07.780
and stretching training program,

01:39:07.780 --> 01:39:09.920
you don't need to push
to the point of pain.

01:39:09.920 --> 01:39:12.620
In fact, it seems that
even just approaching

01:39:12.620 --> 01:39:15.120
the point of pain is
going to be less effective

01:39:15.120 --> 01:39:20.120
than operating at this 30-40% of intensity

01:39:20.400 --> 01:39:21.980
prior to reaching that pain threshold.

01:39:21.980 --> 01:39:23.620
The pain threshold being 100%.

01:39:23.620 --> 01:39:25.120
Now, of course, this is pretty subjective,

01:39:25.120 --> 01:39:27.290
but I think all of us should be able

01:39:27.290 --> 01:39:29.060
to register within ourselves,

01:39:29.060 --> 01:39:31.590
so whether a given range of motion

01:39:31.590 --> 01:39:33.010
or extending a given range of motion

01:39:33.010 --> 01:39:35.770
brings us to that threshold
of pain or near pain.

01:39:35.770 --> 01:39:37.620
And according to this study at least,

01:39:39.130 --> 01:39:41.640
operating or performing stretching

01:39:41.640 --> 01:39:44.770
at an intensity that's quite
low, that's very relaxing

01:39:44.770 --> 01:39:46.960
turns out to be more beneficial

01:39:46.960 --> 01:39:48.570
in increasing range of motion

01:39:48.570 --> 01:39:52.840
than is doing exercises aimed
at increasing range of motion

01:39:52.840 --> 01:39:54.410
at a higher intensity.

01:39:54.410 --> 01:39:56.810
Okay, so lower intensity stretching,

01:39:56.810 --> 01:39:59.380
I should say lower
intensity static stretching

01:39:59.380 --> 01:40:01.480
appears to be the most beneficial way

01:40:01.480 --> 01:40:02.313
to approach stretching.

01:40:02.313 --> 01:40:05.630
And I think that's a relief
probably to many of us

01:40:05.630 --> 01:40:08.760
because it also suggests
that the injury risk

01:40:08.760 --> 01:40:10.810
is going to be lower
than if one were pushing

01:40:10.810 --> 01:40:13.000
into the pain zone, so to speak.

01:40:13.000 --> 01:40:15.210
The authors offer a number
of different explanations

01:40:15.210 --> 01:40:17.940
as to why this approach, this
Microstretching approach,

01:40:17.940 --> 01:40:19.163
might be more effective.

01:40:20.130 --> 01:40:22.460
Here, I'm paraphrasing
from their discussion

01:40:22.460 --> 01:40:25.810
where they mentioned that
it could be hypothesized

01:40:25.810 --> 01:40:28.670
that they had improved
reciprocal inhibition

01:40:28.670 --> 01:40:29.890
within the hamstring muscle group.

01:40:29.890 --> 01:40:31.990
So, this gets right back to
the sorts of neural mechanisms

01:40:31.990 --> 01:40:32.940
that we talked about before,

01:40:32.940 --> 01:40:35.810
that somehow by doing this
low-intensity stretching

01:40:35.810 --> 01:40:37.760
that they were able to
access some of those spindle

01:40:37.760 --> 01:40:41.030
and GTO-type mechanisms that
we were referring to earlier,

01:40:41.030 --> 01:40:44.630
and the inhibition of hamstring
and quadricep stretches.

01:40:44.630 --> 01:40:47.160
They also offer a number
of different ideas

01:40:47.160 --> 01:40:50.670
about how this could shift the activation

01:40:50.670 --> 01:40:51.870
of the so-called sympathetic,

01:40:51.870 --> 01:40:55.550
remember the kind of stress
division of our nervous system,

01:40:55.550 --> 01:40:58.680
and to reduce that relative

01:40:58.680 --> 01:41:00.760
to activation of the parasympathetic arm

01:41:00.760 --> 01:41:01.970
of the nervous system.

01:41:01.970 --> 01:41:03.690
I confess, they have a couple of arguments

01:41:03.690 --> 01:41:05.330
around sympathetic, parasympathetic

01:41:05.330 --> 01:41:06.950
that are somewhat convoluted.

01:41:06.950 --> 01:41:10.940
I will just in fairness
to the neuroscience

01:41:10.940 --> 01:41:11.983
on those systems,

01:41:13.040 --> 01:41:15.300
I wouldn't suggest putting too much weight

01:41:15.300 --> 01:41:18.020
on their arguments about
sympathetic and parasympathetic.

01:41:18.020 --> 01:41:20.370
To my mind, they didn't
really hold much water,

01:41:20.370 --> 01:41:22.380
but here I'm not trying to be disparaging

01:41:22.380 --> 01:41:25.010
of the overall work, which I
think is really quite sound,

01:41:25.010 --> 01:41:28.530
which is that low intensity,
so called Microstretching,

01:41:28.530 --> 01:41:30.410
is going to be the most effective way

01:41:30.410 --> 01:41:33.250
to increase limb range
of movement over time.

01:41:33.250 --> 01:41:35.260
I want to just briefly return to this idea

01:41:35.260 --> 01:41:37.930
of whether or not to do
ballistic or static stretching

01:41:37.930 --> 01:41:40.500
before some sort of skill training,

01:41:40.500 --> 01:41:43.760
or weight training, or any kind of sport,

01:41:43.760 --> 01:41:46.343
or even cardiovascular
exercise like running.

01:41:47.250 --> 01:41:49.290
Again, the data are
really split out there.

01:41:49.290 --> 01:41:50.980
There are even folks who suggest

01:41:50.980 --> 01:41:53.500
that doing any kind of
stretching prior to running

01:41:53.500 --> 01:41:55.250
is going to lower running efficiency,

01:41:55.250 --> 01:41:56.907
it's going to require
essentially more work

01:41:56.907 --> 01:41:59.230
and more oxygen uptake at a given speed

01:42:00.100 --> 01:42:01.220
for a variety of reasons,

01:42:01.220 --> 01:42:04.700
and runners and that community
argue about this endlessly.

01:42:04.700 --> 01:42:06.810
There are papers in both
sides, in both directions,

01:42:06.810 --> 01:42:09.070
I'm sure I'll hear about
some of this in the comments.

01:42:09.070 --> 01:42:12.180
I'm not really going to take a
stance on this as a consequence

01:42:12.180 --> 01:42:14.600
because the data are all over the place.

01:42:14.600 --> 01:42:17.560
However, I think there's a
general logic that we can apply,

01:42:17.560 --> 01:42:20.440
and here I'm borrowing
from some conversations

01:42:20.440 --> 01:42:23.250
and some information put out
there by Dr. Andy Galpin,

01:42:23.250 --> 01:42:25.980
who I think is, of course, both an expert

01:42:25.980 --> 01:42:26.813
and thinks about these things

01:42:26.813 --> 01:42:30.533
in a really sound and
flexible way, no pun intended.

01:42:31.880 --> 01:42:34.950
There are instances, for example,

01:42:34.950 --> 01:42:37.750
where an individual might want
to do some static stretching

01:42:37.750 --> 01:42:39.560
to increase limb range of motion

01:42:39.560 --> 01:42:41.160
prior to doing weight training

01:42:41.160 --> 01:42:44.710
even if it's going to
inhibit that person's ability

01:42:44.710 --> 01:42:46.360
to lift as much weight.

01:42:46.360 --> 01:42:47.390
Why would you want to do that?

01:42:47.390 --> 01:42:48.620
Well, for instance,

01:42:48.620 --> 01:42:50.980
if somebody has a
tightness or a limitation

01:42:50.980 --> 01:42:53.600
in their neuromuscular
connective tissue system

01:42:53.600 --> 01:42:56.620
someplace in their body and system

01:42:56.620 --> 01:43:00.040
that prevents them from using proper form,

01:43:00.040 --> 01:43:03.750
that they can overcome by
doing some static stretching.

01:43:03.750 --> 01:43:05.510
Well, that would be a great idea

01:43:05.510 --> 01:43:07.640
as Dr. Galpin points out.

01:43:07.640 --> 01:43:11.510
Or for instance, if proper
stability within the movement

01:43:11.510 --> 01:43:14.570
requires increasing limb
range of motion in some way,

01:43:14.570 --> 01:43:18.750
well then compromising
the use of greater loads

01:43:18.750 --> 01:43:22.050
could be greatly offset by
doing some static stretching

01:43:22.050 --> 01:43:23.880
to improve, say, hamstring flexibility

01:43:23.880 --> 01:43:25.600
or another muscle group flexibility.

01:43:25.600 --> 01:43:26.610
So, we can't always think about

01:43:26.610 --> 01:43:28.370
just what's going to allow us

01:43:28.370 --> 01:43:31.010
or inhibit us from using
the maximal amount of weight

01:43:31.010 --> 01:43:33.720
or from running as far as we want to run

01:43:33.720 --> 01:43:35.260
as fast as we want to run.

01:43:35.260 --> 01:43:37.390
There are instances where
people are trying to overcome

01:43:37.390 --> 01:43:39.960
injuries where they're trying to come back

01:43:39.960 --> 01:43:41.870
from a repetitive surgery

01:43:41.870 --> 01:43:44.080
or something of that sort,
coming back from a layoff

01:43:44.080 --> 01:43:47.130
where some additional static stretching

01:43:47.130 --> 01:43:49.300
prior to cardiovascular,
or weight training,

01:43:49.300 --> 01:43:51.170
or skill training, or sport of some kind

01:43:51.170 --> 01:43:54.040
is going to be useful
because it's going to put us

01:43:54.040 --> 01:43:55.660
in a position of greater safety

01:43:55.660 --> 01:43:58.220
and confidence and performance overall,

01:43:58.220 --> 01:44:00.720
even if it's adjusting down our speed

01:44:00.720 --> 01:44:02.920
or the total amount of loads that we use.

01:44:02.920 --> 01:44:05.640
So, it's you that needs to consider

01:44:05.640 --> 01:44:09.450
whether or not for you and
within a given training session

01:44:09.450 --> 01:44:12.190
you want to do static training,

01:44:12.190 --> 01:44:15.010
I should say static stretching
range of motion training

01:44:15.010 --> 01:44:18.570
prior to or after that training session.

01:44:18.570 --> 01:44:22.180
And similarly, there are a lot of data

01:44:22.180 --> 01:44:24.160
pointing to the fact
that doing some dynamic

01:44:24.160 --> 01:44:27.450
or even ballistic stretching
prior to skill training,

01:44:27.450 --> 01:44:30.000
or cardiovascular, or weight
training can be beneficial

01:44:30.000 --> 01:44:32.410
in part to warm-up the
relevant neural circuits,

01:44:32.410 --> 01:44:35.040
joints, and connective
tissue, and muscles,

01:44:35.040 --> 01:44:38.800
and as well to perhaps
improve range of motion

01:44:38.800 --> 01:44:42.160
or ability to perform those
movements more accurately,

01:44:42.160 --> 01:44:45.200
with more stability, and
therefore with more confidence.

01:44:45.200 --> 01:44:46.870
And while Dr. Andy Galpin

01:44:46.870 --> 01:44:49.240
would never name any
protocol after himself,

01:44:49.240 --> 01:44:50.750
he's far too humble to do that,

01:44:50.750 --> 01:44:52.810
I've named a couple of
protocols after him,

01:44:52.810 --> 01:44:55.660
particularly the Galpin
equation for hydration,

01:44:55.660 --> 01:44:58.040
because he was willing to
stick his neck out there

01:44:58.040 --> 01:45:02.320
and put down some specific
numbers that people could follow

01:45:02.320 --> 01:45:04.950
in order to ensure proper
hydration during training,

01:45:04.950 --> 01:45:07.330
you can look up the
Galpin equation elsewhere.

01:45:07.330 --> 01:45:10.720
You can just Google it or
look elsewhere and find it.

01:45:10.720 --> 01:45:15.290
And Dr. Galpin has also been
very thoughtful and generous,

01:45:15.290 --> 01:45:17.850
and I think very accurate in offering

01:45:17.850 --> 01:45:20.640
a kind of a general organizational logic

01:45:20.640 --> 01:45:22.900
for how to think about the goals

01:45:22.900 --> 01:45:25.320
of a particular training session,

01:45:25.320 --> 01:45:27.820
and thereby to decide whether or not

01:45:27.820 --> 01:45:29.730
you're going to do ballistic,
or static stretching,

01:45:29.730 --> 01:45:31.090
and so on and so forth.

01:45:31.090 --> 01:45:35.843
So, we can refer to this
general approach as Galpinian.

01:45:35.843 --> 01:45:37.530
Galpinian, is that right?

01:45:37.530 --> 01:45:41.600
Galpin-ian logic, Galpinian logic.

01:45:41.600 --> 01:45:43.270
Thus far, we've been
talking about stretching

01:45:43.270 --> 01:45:46.540
for sake of increasing limb
flexibility and range of motion,

01:45:46.540 --> 01:45:49.550
but there are other reasons perhaps

01:45:49.550 --> 01:45:52.150
to embark on a stretching protocol

01:45:52.150 --> 01:45:55.780
that include both our ability to relax

01:45:55.780 --> 01:45:58.160
and access deep relaxation quickly,

01:45:58.160 --> 01:46:01.710
as well as even to reduce inflammation

01:46:01.710 --> 01:46:04.650
and perhaps even combat
certain forms of cancer.

01:46:04.650 --> 01:46:06.900
And if that sounds really far-fetched,

01:46:06.900 --> 01:46:08.780
I want to emphasize that the study

01:46:08.780 --> 01:46:10.490
I'm about to share with you in a moment

01:46:10.490 --> 01:46:13.250
was actually carried out
by one of the directors

01:46:13.250 --> 01:46:17.450
of a division of the National
Institutes of Health.

01:46:17.450 --> 01:46:21.140
And this was the work of Helene Langevin,

01:46:21.140 --> 01:46:24.480
who's a medical doctor, has
done really important work

01:46:25.320 --> 01:46:30.010
on the mechanisms underlying
things like acupuncture

01:46:30.010 --> 01:46:31.290
and has approached all that

01:46:31.290 --> 01:46:34.020
from a very mechanistic viewpoint.

01:46:34.020 --> 01:46:36.460
Right, so not looking just at
the effects of acupuncture,

01:46:36.460 --> 01:46:39.450
but really trying to understand
what sorts of cytokines,

01:46:39.450 --> 01:46:43.040
inflammatory molecules,
and pathways are activated?

01:46:43.040 --> 01:46:46.030
What sorts of neural
mechanisms get engaged

01:46:46.030 --> 01:46:48.260
by things like acupuncture

01:46:48.260 --> 01:46:52.200
that impinges on the fascial
tissues and so forth?

01:46:52.200 --> 01:46:53.690
And Dr. Langevin is currently

01:46:53.690 --> 01:46:55.420
Director of the National Institutes

01:46:55.420 --> 01:46:59.360
of Complementary Health and Medicine

01:46:59.360 --> 01:47:00.700
at the National Institutes of Health.

01:47:00.700 --> 01:47:03.730
So, this is a major division
supported by tax dollars

01:47:03.730 --> 01:47:07.190
that support systematic
mechanistic exploration

01:47:07.190 --> 01:47:11.430
of things like respiration,
meditation, yoga, acupuncture.

01:47:11.430 --> 01:47:14.380
So, this is serious science
applied to protocols

01:47:14.380 --> 01:47:16.130
and approaches that have been
used for some period of time,

01:47:16.130 --> 01:47:17.640
but really aimed at trying to understand

01:47:17.640 --> 01:47:21.700
what would the best protocols
be to evolve new protocols?

01:47:21.700 --> 01:47:23.830
So, there's a really interesting study

01:47:23.830 --> 01:47:25.310
done in animal models,

01:47:25.310 --> 01:47:27.510
but I think it's a powerful enough result

01:47:27.510 --> 01:47:29.660
that I think we all should
pay attention to it.

01:47:29.660 --> 01:47:30.750
The title of this paper,

01:47:30.750 --> 01:47:33.760
and, again, the last author
is Dr. Langevin herself,

01:47:33.760 --> 01:47:36.470
is Stretching Reduces Tumor Growth

01:47:36.470 --> 01:47:38.630
in a Mouse Breast Cancer Model.

01:47:38.630 --> 01:47:40.620
And yes, you can get mice to stretch,

01:47:40.620 --> 01:47:44.070
it turns out that if you gently
lift up mice by their tail

01:47:44.070 --> 01:47:45.050
and they'll hold onto their cage,

01:47:45.050 --> 01:47:47.460
there's a way in which you
can mechanically stretch them

01:47:47.460 --> 01:47:48.900
in a way that doesn't harm them.

01:47:48.900 --> 01:47:51.290
First, I should mention
that Dr. Langevin and others

01:47:51.290 --> 01:47:54.960
have shown that just a brief
whole body stretch of that sort

01:47:54.960 --> 01:47:59.960
induces an increase in activation
of the parasympathetic arm

01:48:00.220 --> 01:48:01.940
of the autonomic nervous system,

01:48:01.940 --> 01:48:03.510
again, not arm, limb arm,

01:48:03.510 --> 01:48:05.880
but the aspect of the
autonomic nervous system

01:48:05.880 --> 01:48:08.170
that creates a whole body,

01:48:08.170 --> 01:48:11.870
whole nervous system shift
toward more relaxation.

01:48:11.870 --> 01:48:14.630
So, yes, indeed stretching

01:48:14.630 --> 01:48:18.060
induces relaxation at a systemic level,

01:48:18.060 --> 01:48:19.410
not just at a local level.

01:48:19.410 --> 01:48:21.130
And I think that's important,

01:48:21.130 --> 01:48:22.680
probably not surprising to those of you

01:48:22.680 --> 01:48:24.300
that use stretching regularly,

01:48:24.300 --> 01:48:26.370
but yes, it does indeed relax us.

01:48:26.370 --> 01:48:29.620
Yes, you can do this in mice
and see that in mice as well.

01:48:29.620 --> 01:48:31.980
Here's what they did
for this current study,

01:48:31.980 --> 01:48:35.710
or I should say this was
a study published in 2018

01:48:35.710 --> 01:48:37.053
in Scientific Reports.

01:48:38.790 --> 01:48:40.900
They write, "Recent studies have shown

01:48:40.900 --> 01:48:43.140
that gentle daily stretching
for 10 minutes can reduce

01:48:43.140 --> 01:48:45.410
local connective tissue
inflammation and fibrosis."

01:48:45.410 --> 01:48:49.740
Now, that's local tissue
inflammation and fibrosis

01:48:49.740 --> 01:48:53.810
as well we now know as
systemic inflammation

01:48:53.810 --> 01:48:56.823
and can induce relaxation systemically.

01:48:57.770 --> 01:49:00.880
In this case, they focused
on mice, not humans.

01:49:00.880 --> 01:49:02.680
And mice were randomized to a stretch

01:49:02.680 --> 01:49:04.250
versus no stretch condition

01:49:04.250 --> 01:49:08.450
and were treated for 10 minutes
once a day for four weeks.

01:49:08.450 --> 01:49:11.720
So, it's 10 minutes of this
passive whole body stretching

01:49:12.600 --> 01:49:14.720
a day for four weeks.

01:49:14.720 --> 01:49:16.190
What's remarkable, I mean,

01:49:16.190 --> 01:49:18.390
just I have to say is just striking

01:49:18.390 --> 01:49:20.470
is that tumor volume in these mice,

01:49:20.470 --> 01:49:22.130
they were able to induce
tumors in these mice

01:49:22.130 --> 01:49:26.020
and the tumor volume at the
end point was 52% smaller

01:49:26.020 --> 01:49:29.320
in the stretch group compared
to the no stretch group.

01:49:29.320 --> 01:49:31.610
This is a highly significant effect,

01:49:31.610 --> 01:49:34.960
and they point out in the
absence of any other treatment.

01:49:34.960 --> 01:49:37.740
And they explored whether or
not cytotoxic immune responses

01:49:37.740 --> 01:49:40.530
were activated and a
number of other features.

01:49:40.530 --> 01:49:41.760
They weren't able to get too deeply

01:49:41.760 --> 01:49:43.930
into the underlying mechanisms,

01:49:43.930 --> 01:49:45.580
but this is pretty remarkable.

01:49:45.580 --> 01:49:48.010
Even three weeks into stretching protocol,

01:49:48.010 --> 01:49:49.830
this daily stretching
protocol for these mice

01:49:49.830 --> 01:49:54.090
tumor volume was reduced, I
mean, by it's almost halved,

01:49:54.090 --> 01:49:55.660
this is pretty incredible.

01:49:55.660 --> 01:49:57.860
So, they have these
measures of tumor volume

01:49:58.770 --> 01:50:01.370
and the only difference
in the way these animals

01:50:01.370 --> 01:50:03.010
were treated and handled

01:50:03.010 --> 01:50:06.260
was the introduction
of this daily stretch.

01:50:06.260 --> 01:50:09.730
I find this result to
be, of course, limited

01:50:09.730 --> 01:50:12.190
in to the extent that it's
done in an animal model,

01:50:12.190 --> 01:50:14.140
not in humans, we have to point it out,

01:50:14.140 --> 01:50:16.157
but as they point out in their discussion,

01:50:16.157 --> 01:50:18.330
"Our results demonstrate a 52% reduction

01:50:18.330 --> 01:50:20.450
in mammary tumor growth over one month

01:50:20.450 --> 01:50:22.600
in mice undergoing stretching
for 10 minutes a day

01:50:22.600 --> 01:50:24.700
without any other form of therapy."

01:50:24.700 --> 01:50:27.040
Do they think that stretching itself

01:50:27.040 --> 01:50:28.540
is changing the tumor size?

01:50:28.540 --> 01:50:31.870
No, in fact they raise the
possibility that stretching

01:50:31.870 --> 01:50:33.740
because of its impact on the fascia

01:50:33.740 --> 01:50:35.430
might even create micro environments

01:50:35.430 --> 01:50:37.770
that are more permissive for tumor growth

01:50:37.770 --> 01:50:38.870
in certain instances,

01:50:38.870 --> 01:50:41.280
so they're careful to emphasize

01:50:41.280 --> 01:50:42.920
what I also believe to be the case,

01:50:42.920 --> 01:50:45.050
which is that it's unlikely
that the stretching itself

01:50:45.050 --> 01:50:47.610
was directly acting to reduce tumor size,

01:50:47.610 --> 01:50:50.360
but rather that there's this possible link

01:50:50.360 --> 01:50:53.620
between inflammation and
immune exhaustion mechanisms

01:50:53.620 --> 01:50:58.070
that if you can periodically
relax a nervous system,

01:50:58.070 --> 01:50:59.320
here through stretching,

01:50:59.320 --> 01:51:02.330
that it can affect certain pathways

01:51:02.330 --> 01:51:03.450
related to the immune system

01:51:03.450 --> 01:51:06.570
that would allow the immune
system to combat tumor growth

01:51:06.570 --> 01:51:07.840
to a significant degree.

01:51:07.840 --> 01:51:10.100
So, again, even though
this is a study in mice,

01:51:10.100 --> 01:51:12.900
it argues that relaxation
induced by stretching

01:51:12.900 --> 01:51:15.560
can have a powerful influence
on mammary tumor growth.

01:51:15.560 --> 01:51:18.150
Again, a huge effect carried out

01:51:18.150 --> 01:51:21.970
by one of the premier labs and individuals

01:51:22.810 --> 01:51:25.090
who do this sort of work and
think about this sort of thing.

01:51:25.090 --> 01:51:26.100
And, of course, I want to point out

01:51:26.100 --> 01:51:28.150
it wasn't just Dr. Langevin
that did this study,

01:51:28.150 --> 01:51:29.680
there are a number of
co-authors on the study

01:51:29.680 --> 01:51:32.170
we will provide a link to the co-authors,

01:51:32.170 --> 01:51:34.330
excuse me, we will provide
a link to the study

01:51:34.330 --> 01:51:37.160
so that you can peruse it
in more detail if you like.

01:51:37.160 --> 01:51:40.490
Now, as a related and
somewhat final point,

01:51:40.490 --> 01:51:43.550
I'd like to return to
this idea and this place,

01:51:43.550 --> 01:51:45.530
this real estate within our brain

01:51:45.530 --> 01:51:48.020
that we call the insular
cortex, the insula.

01:51:48.020 --> 01:51:51.190
As you recall, way back at
the beginning of this episode,

01:51:51.190 --> 01:51:53.050
we were talking about
the von Economo neurons,

01:51:53.050 --> 01:51:54.750
that Constantin von Economo

01:51:54.750 --> 01:51:58.150
the Austrian scientist discovered.

01:51:58.150 --> 01:52:01.320
And the fact that we are able to make

01:52:01.320 --> 01:52:05.580
and perform interpretations of
our internal landscape pain,

01:52:05.580 --> 01:52:08.020
our dedication to a practice,

01:52:08.020 --> 01:52:10.870
for instance, whether
or not we are in pain

01:52:10.870 --> 01:52:14.140
because it's a practice that
we are doing intentionally

01:52:14.140 --> 01:52:15.570
and want to improve ourselves,

01:52:15.570 --> 01:52:17.440
or whether or not it's
pain that's arriving

01:52:17.440 --> 01:52:20.920
through some externally
imposed demands or situations.

01:52:20.920 --> 01:52:23.440
Well, the insula is handling all that.

01:52:23.440 --> 01:52:26.160
And fortunately, there's a wonderful paper

01:52:26.160 --> 01:52:27.280
that was published,

01:52:27.280 --> 01:52:30.140
it was a few years ago now in
the journal Cerebral Cortex,

01:52:30.140 --> 01:52:31.420
which is a fine journal.

01:52:31.420 --> 01:52:33.190
This is the year 2014

01:52:33.190 --> 01:52:36.020
entitled Insular Cortex Mediates
Increased Pain Tolerance

01:52:36.020 --> 01:52:37.410
in Yoga Practitioners.

01:52:37.410 --> 01:52:39.250
I'll tell you why I like this study.

01:52:39.250 --> 01:52:41.150
I'm personally not a practitioner of yoga,

01:52:41.150 --> 01:52:42.970
I've taken a few yoga
classes over the years,

01:52:42.970 --> 01:52:44.920
I've done some of the hot yoga classes.

01:52:45.910 --> 01:52:49.526
Those rooms can get really,
really warm, I confess,

01:52:49.526 --> 01:52:51.870
and I've done the kind of
standard yoga every now and again.

01:52:51.870 --> 01:52:54.220
It's not something that
I've kept up regularly.

01:52:55.210 --> 01:52:58.850
This study explored the effects

01:52:58.850 --> 01:53:03.730
on brain structure volume
in yoga practitioners.

01:53:03.730 --> 01:53:06.620
And for those of you out there
that are aficionados in yoga,

01:53:06.620 --> 01:53:11.430
they pulled subjects from
having backgrounds in the...

01:53:11.430 --> 01:53:12.750
Here, I'm probably going to mispronounce

01:53:12.750 --> 01:53:14.480
these different things, and forgive me,

01:53:14.480 --> 01:53:17.118
the Vinysasa yogas, the Ashtanga yogas,

01:53:17.118 --> 01:53:19.350
the Iyengar yogas, the Sivananda yogas.

01:53:19.350 --> 01:53:21.630
Okay, so some people were
new to these practices,

01:53:21.630 --> 01:53:23.800
some were experienced.

01:53:23.800 --> 01:53:25.620
That the important take aways

01:53:25.620 --> 01:53:28.200
were that they took
these yoga practitioners

01:53:28.200 --> 01:53:30.290
and they didn't explore
their brain structure

01:53:30.290 --> 01:53:31.600
in the context of yoga itself,

01:53:31.600 --> 01:53:34.480
they looked at things like pain tolerance.

01:53:34.480 --> 01:53:36.500
So, they used thermal stimulation,

01:53:36.500 --> 01:53:37.980
basically they put people into conditions

01:53:37.980 --> 01:53:40.370
where they gave them very
hot or very cold stimuli

01:53:40.370 --> 01:53:43.160
and compared those yoga practitioners

01:53:43.160 --> 01:53:44.980
of varying levels of yoga experience

01:53:44.980 --> 01:53:47.290
to those that had no experience with yoga,

01:53:47.290 --> 01:53:48.420
so-called controls.

01:53:48.420 --> 01:53:50.470
And they found some
really interesting things,

01:53:50.470 --> 01:53:52.810
there are a lot of data in this paper,

01:53:52.810 --> 01:53:55.790
but here's something
I'd like to highlight.

01:53:55.790 --> 01:53:59.260
The pain tolerance of yoga practitioners

01:53:59.260 --> 01:54:04.260
was double or more to that
of non-yoga practitioners,

01:54:04.270 --> 01:54:06.670
even for those that weren't
doing this so-called hot yoga.

01:54:06.670 --> 01:54:09.530
Right, they also found that pain tolerance

01:54:09.530 --> 01:54:10.600
was significantly greater,

01:54:10.600 --> 01:54:13.330
both for heat pain and for cold pain.

01:54:13.330 --> 01:54:17.603
They also found significant
increases in insula,

01:54:18.830 --> 01:54:20.740
again, the insula, this brain region,

01:54:20.740 --> 01:54:21.930
gray matter volume.

01:54:21.930 --> 01:54:23.440
Typically, when we talk about gray matter,

01:54:23.440 --> 01:54:25.400
we're talking about the
so-called cell bodies,

01:54:25.400 --> 01:54:29.970
the location in neurons
where the genome is housed

01:54:29.970 --> 01:54:33.080
and where the kind of all the
housekeeping stuff is there.

01:54:33.080 --> 01:54:36.040
And then white matter volume
tends to be the axons,

01:54:36.040 --> 01:54:38.270
the wires because they're
ensheathed with this stuff

01:54:38.270 --> 01:54:40.270
that appears white in MRIs,

01:54:40.270 --> 01:54:42.040
and indeed is white under the microscope,

01:54:42.040 --> 01:54:44.740
and indeed is white, it's
actually lipid, which is myelin.

01:54:44.740 --> 01:54:48.300
So, increased gray matter
volume of the insula

01:54:48.300 --> 01:54:50.470
is a significant finding

01:54:50.470 --> 01:54:53.610
because what it suggests is
that people that are doing yoga

01:54:53.610 --> 01:54:56.183
have an increased volume
of these areas of the brain

01:54:56.183 --> 01:54:59.120
that are associated with
interoceptive awareness

01:54:59.120 --> 01:55:02.100
and for being able to
make judgements about pain

01:55:02.100 --> 01:55:03.960
and why one is experiencing pain,

01:55:03.960 --> 01:55:05.290
not just to lean away from pain,

01:55:05.290 --> 01:55:08.700
but to utilize or leverage
or even overcome pain.

01:55:08.700 --> 01:55:12.990
So, there are many studies of
yoga and meditation out there,

01:55:12.990 --> 01:55:15.970
few that have as much
mechanistic detail as this one.

01:55:15.970 --> 01:55:18.430
And in fact, there's a beautiful figure,

01:55:18.430 --> 01:55:19.930
Figure 3 in this paper

01:55:19.930 --> 01:55:21.740
that shows that the gray matter volume

01:55:21.740 --> 01:55:23.600
of this particular brain region

01:55:23.600 --> 01:55:26.560
scales in a almost linear way

01:55:26.560 --> 01:55:28.350
with the duration of yoga practice

01:55:28.350 --> 01:55:30.020
that somebody has been taking on in years.

01:55:30.020 --> 01:55:32.360
So, people that had, well,
hey had a few subjects

01:55:32.360 --> 01:55:35.370
that have up to 15 or 16
years of yoga practice

01:55:35.370 --> 01:55:39.290
had much larger left
insula gray matter volume,

01:55:39.290 --> 01:55:42.710
bigger brain areas associated
with these abilities.

01:55:42.710 --> 01:55:44.230
And I find this interesting

01:55:44.230 --> 01:55:46.910
because there are a lot
of activities out there

01:55:46.910 --> 01:55:50.600
that don't create these kind
of changes in brain volume,

01:55:50.600 --> 01:55:52.080
especially within the insula.

01:55:52.080 --> 01:55:54.420
So, it appears that it's
not just the performance

01:55:54.420 --> 01:55:55.810
of the yogic movements,

01:55:55.810 --> 01:55:57.880
but the overcoming or the kind of pushing

01:55:57.880 --> 01:55:59.890
into the end ranges of motion

01:55:59.890 --> 01:56:03.470
and to push through
discomfort to some extent,

01:56:03.470 --> 01:56:06.770
of course, we want people doing
that in a healthy, safe way,

01:56:06.770 --> 01:56:08.700
but that allows yoga practitioners

01:56:08.700 --> 01:56:12.390
to build up the structure and function

01:56:12.390 --> 01:56:14.960
of these brain areas that
allow them to cope with pain

01:56:14.960 --> 01:56:16.740
better than other individuals,

01:56:16.740 --> 01:56:20.410
and to cope with other kinds
of interoceptive challenges,

01:56:20.410 --> 01:56:23.236
if you will, not just pain but cold,

01:56:23.236 --> 01:56:25.300
not just pain but discomfort

01:56:25.300 --> 01:56:28.000
of being in a particular
position to do that.

01:56:28.000 --> 01:56:30.300
And again, we wouldn't want
people placing themselves

01:56:30.300 --> 01:56:33.930
into a compromised position
literally that would harm them,

01:56:33.930 --> 01:56:36.400
especially given that earlier we heard

01:56:36.400 --> 01:56:38.850
that Microstretching of
the kind of non-painful

01:56:38.850 --> 01:56:41.240
sort low-intensity sort is
actually going to be more effective

01:56:41.240 --> 01:56:43.010
for increasing end range of motion.

01:56:43.010 --> 01:56:45.610
But this study really
emphasizes the extent to which

01:56:46.720 --> 01:56:50.010
practitioners of yoga
don't just learn movements,

01:56:50.010 --> 01:56:53.020
they learn how to control
their nervous system in ways

01:56:53.020 --> 01:56:57.010
that really reshapes their
relationship to pain,

01:56:57.010 --> 01:56:59.940
to flexibility, and to the kinds of things

01:56:59.940 --> 01:57:03.230
that the neuromuscular
system was designed to do.

01:57:03.230 --> 01:57:04.960
And as a final point,

01:57:04.960 --> 01:57:07.390
there's a beautiful graph in this paper,

01:57:07.390 --> 01:57:08.980
beautiful I think because it explores

01:57:08.980 --> 01:57:13.160
some of the more subjective
dimensions of yoga

01:57:13.160 --> 01:57:15.070
and insula function,

01:57:15.070 --> 01:57:18.590
which is a here I'll read
it out in the nerdy form

01:57:18.590 --> 01:57:19.887
and then I'll explain what it means,

01:57:19.887 --> 01:57:21.600
"This is a frequency histogram

01:57:21.600 --> 01:57:24.860
of categories of mental
strategies used by yogis

01:57:24.860 --> 01:57:27.770
versus controls during the
cold pain tolerance task."

01:57:27.770 --> 01:57:30.190
What they're describing here and showing

01:57:30.190 --> 01:57:35.190
is quantitatively how
people are conceptualizing

01:57:35.470 --> 01:57:37.870
cold pain in order to get through it.

01:57:37.870 --> 01:57:40.430
And the different categories
are, for instance,

01:57:40.430 --> 01:57:41.690
distraction, right?

01:57:41.690 --> 01:57:44.550
Some people just choose to
distract themselves from pain

01:57:44.550 --> 01:57:46.910
or to attempt to, other
people will try to ignore it,

01:57:46.910 --> 01:57:48.220
it's a lot like distraction,

01:57:48.220 --> 01:57:53.140
but nonetheless, to engage
in a negative emotion,

01:57:53.140 --> 01:57:54.720
sort of like, "[growls]
like I'm going to dig,

01:57:54.720 --> 01:57:56.737
I'm going to be in resistance to this."

01:57:57.940 --> 01:58:00.780
Control subjects tended
to use those approaches,

01:58:00.780 --> 01:58:04.060
whereas practitioners
of yoga tended to use

01:58:04.060 --> 01:58:07.580
other sorts of subjective
approaches like positive imagery

01:58:07.580 --> 01:58:09.150
to some extent,

01:58:09.150 --> 01:58:13.540
the ability to relax
despite the extreme cold,

01:58:13.540 --> 01:58:15.980
the ability to quote-unquote accept

01:58:15.980 --> 01:58:18.830
like, "This is just happening,"
despite the extreme cold,

01:58:18.830 --> 01:58:21.290
to observe, to third person themselves.

01:58:21.290 --> 01:58:24.990
And the greatest effect,
of course, was to breathe,

01:58:24.990 --> 01:58:27.150
to focus on their respiration

01:58:27.150 --> 01:58:31.420
as a way to deal with this
challenge, this cold challenge.

01:58:31.420 --> 01:58:34.300
Now, all of that are subjective data,

01:58:34.300 --> 01:58:38.510
but I want to remind you that
the practitioners of yoga

01:58:38.510 --> 01:58:41.370
are not just using entirely
different mental strategies,

01:58:41.370 --> 01:58:45.000
but they are far more
effective at dealing with pain,

01:58:45.000 --> 01:58:47.890
their pain tolerance is much higher

01:58:47.890 --> 01:58:49.430
as evidenced by the other data

01:58:49.430 --> 01:58:51.390
in the previous graphs in the paper.

01:58:51.390 --> 01:58:53.610
So, while this podcast episode

01:58:53.610 --> 01:58:56.000
is most certainly not about yoga per se,

01:58:56.000 --> 01:58:58.630
it's about flexibility and stretching,

01:58:58.630 --> 01:59:00.280
flexibility and stretching are elements

01:59:00.280 --> 01:59:02.390
within yogic practices.

01:59:02.390 --> 01:59:03.720
And, of course, yogic practices

01:59:03.720 --> 01:59:06.190
involve breathing and mental work,

01:59:06.190 --> 01:59:08.440
and a lot of other things
balance, et cetera.

01:59:08.440 --> 01:59:11.260
It's a vast landscape
as as many of you know.

01:59:11.260 --> 01:59:14.440
But I think that if ever
there was a manuscript

01:59:14.440 --> 01:59:17.830
that pointed to the utility
of something like yoga

01:59:17.830 --> 01:59:21.980
for sake of tapping into a
particular set of brain circuits

01:59:21.980 --> 01:59:24.240
and mechanisms that could wick out

01:59:24.240 --> 01:59:26.520
into multiple dimensions of life,

01:59:26.520 --> 01:59:29.480
so day-to-day life, stress,

01:59:29.480 --> 01:59:32.520
challenges in dealing with all
sorts of external stressors,

01:59:32.520 --> 01:59:35.990
career-related,
family-related, relationally,

01:59:35.990 --> 01:59:38.430
et cetera, et cetera, excuse me,

01:59:38.430 --> 01:59:41.870
but as well for increasing range of motion

01:59:41.870 --> 01:59:43.370
for increasing flexibility.

01:59:43.370 --> 01:59:46.760
So, if ever there was a practice
that one could embark on

01:59:46.760 --> 01:59:48.737
that would not only increase flexibility

01:59:48.737 --> 01:59:49.980
and limb range of motion,

01:59:49.980 --> 01:59:52.320
but would also allow one to cultivate

01:59:52.320 --> 01:59:54.330
some improved mental functioning

01:59:54.330 --> 01:59:55.830
as it relates to pain tolerance

01:59:55.830 --> 01:59:58.190
and other features of stress management

01:59:58.190 --> 02:00:01.180
that no doubt wick out
into other areas of life,

02:00:01.180 --> 02:00:04.360
appears that yoga is a
quite useful practice.

02:00:04.360 --> 02:00:06.100
And so, for those of
you that are interested

02:00:06.100 --> 02:00:07.610
in increasing limb range of motion

02:00:07.610 --> 02:00:09.800
and you're already a
practitioner of yoga, great.

02:00:09.800 --> 02:00:12.500
I can imagine that someday
there'll be another study

02:00:12.500 --> 02:00:15.010
like this one and you'll be in that 10

02:00:15.010 --> 02:00:18.560
or 15-16 year practitioner graph.

02:00:18.560 --> 02:00:21.820
You'll be that dot way out
on the far end of the graph

02:00:21.820 --> 02:00:23.760
that shows that your
insula is that much bigger

02:00:23.760 --> 02:00:25.050
than the rest of ours,

02:00:25.050 --> 02:00:28.010
and therefore your internal
awareness, and pain thresholds,

02:00:28.010 --> 02:00:30.170
and stress management
will be that much better,

02:00:30.170 --> 02:00:32.920
but of course, yoga isn't the only way

02:00:32.920 --> 02:00:35.763
to increase limb range of
motion and flexibility.

02:00:36.950 --> 02:00:38.070
Up until now, we've described

02:00:38.070 --> 02:00:39.690
a number of different ways to do that

02:00:39.690 --> 02:00:42.200
and we've arrived at some
general themes and protocols.

02:00:42.200 --> 02:00:45.010
Again, those themes and
protocols will be distilled

02:00:45.010 --> 02:00:47.940
into some specific and precise list

02:00:47.940 --> 02:00:49.410
in our Neural Network Newsletter,

02:00:49.410 --> 02:00:51.390
but we can revisit a couple of them now

02:00:51.390 --> 02:00:54.240
just in summary and synthesis.

02:00:54.240 --> 02:00:56.740
Static stretching appears
to be at least among

02:00:56.740 --> 02:00:58.440
the more useful forms of stretching.

02:00:58.440 --> 02:01:01.240
So, low or zero momentum stretching

02:01:01.240 --> 02:01:03.830
typically at end range of motion.

02:01:03.830 --> 02:01:05.780
I love this concept of Microstretching,

02:01:07.110 --> 02:01:08.870
even though it's just a couple of studies

02:01:08.870 --> 02:01:11.150
that have addressed whether
or not high-intensity

02:01:11.150 --> 02:01:14.300
or low-intensity static stretch
holds are more beneficial.

02:01:14.300 --> 02:01:18.190
The idea and indeed the
data that low-intensity,

02:01:18.190 --> 02:01:23.190
so 30-40% of what would
one would consider painful

02:01:23.410 --> 02:01:27.400
appears to be more effective
than 80% of that threshold.

02:01:27.400 --> 02:01:28.780
I find that incredibly interesting.

02:01:28.780 --> 02:01:30.980
And then, there's this idea of frequency,

02:01:30.980 --> 02:01:32.780
it really does appear that getting

02:01:33.720 --> 02:01:36.970
at least five minutes per
week total of stretching

02:01:36.970 --> 02:01:39.450
for a given muscle group
is important for creating

02:01:39.450 --> 02:01:42.770
meaningful, lasting changes
in limb range of motion.

02:01:42.770 --> 02:01:46.590
And that is best achieved
by five day a week,

02:01:46.590 --> 02:01:49.320
or six day a week, or even
seven day a week protocols,

02:01:49.320 --> 02:01:51.410
but those can be very short protocols

02:01:51.410 --> 02:01:54.270
limited to, say, three sets of 30,

02:01:54.270 --> 02:01:57.540
maybe in 45 or 60 seconds of static hold.

02:01:57.540 --> 02:02:01.530
Although, 30 seconds seems
to be a key threshold there

02:02:01.530 --> 02:02:03.750
that can get you maximum benefit.

02:02:03.750 --> 02:02:05.760
There is no need to do
full 60-second holds

02:02:05.760 --> 02:02:08.430
unless you're doing fewer
total sessions per week.

02:02:08.430 --> 02:02:10.580
And, of course, to always warm-up

02:02:10.580 --> 02:02:13.020
or to arrive at the
stretching session warm.

02:02:13.020 --> 02:02:15.280
And then, of course, there are
the other forms of stretching

02:02:15.280 --> 02:02:18.300
that we touched upon a
bit, things like PNF.

02:02:18.300 --> 02:02:20.320
And we talked about why PNF works,

02:02:20.320 --> 02:02:23.670
things like the spindle and
the Golgi tendon organ reflexes

02:02:23.670 --> 02:02:25.000
that are built into all of us

02:02:25.000 --> 02:02:26.750
that we arrive in this world with.

02:02:26.750 --> 02:02:28.410
And of course, the other
forms of stretching

02:02:28.410 --> 02:02:30.970
that are known to be
effective and important,

02:02:30.970 --> 02:02:34.180
such as dynamic and ballistic stretching.

02:02:34.180 --> 02:02:36.460
Again, stretching protocols that involve

02:02:36.460 --> 02:02:39.970
a lot of momentum in order
to improve range of motion

02:02:39.970 --> 02:02:42.880
for performance of
particular types of work

02:02:42.880 --> 02:02:44.750
that one is about to embark on.

02:02:44.750 --> 02:02:46.610
Typically, that would be physical work,

02:02:46.610 --> 02:02:50.400
but a whole interesting
and unexplored landscape

02:02:50.400 --> 02:02:53.770
is the extent to which
changing limb range of motion

02:02:53.770 --> 02:02:55.210
and different types of body movement

02:02:55.210 --> 02:02:57.630
actually shape our cognitive abilities,

02:02:57.630 --> 02:02:59.940
and that will be the
topic of a future episode

